ction between lactaldehyde and the proposed hydantoin precursor. It does not resolve the uncertain lactaldehyde supply, carbon dioxide trapping conditions or terminal hydrothermal conversion. Hydrothermal water alone is not sufficient evidence for useful hydantoin hydrolysis and decarbamoylation, especially when threonine degradation may compete. The surface-to-hydrothermal transfer also remains unexplained.", "approved": false, "problematic_reactions": ["rxn_002", "rxn_009", "rxn_010"], "suggestions": ["Demonstrate rxn_002 and rxn_009 for the actual substrates rather than only analogous nitriles or hydantoins.", "Specify hydrothermal pH, temperature, pressure and residence time, and quantify net threonine accumulation.", "Explain transport, dilution and intermediate survival between the two settings."]}, {"pathway_id": "P5", "reaction_scores": {"rxn_003": 0.55, "rxn_004": 0.91, "rxn_005": 0.82, "rxn_007": 0.75, "rxn_008": 0.73}, "chemical_feasibility": 0.68, "pathway_coherence": 0.83, "environmental_progression": 0.6, "target_achievement": 0.7, "overall_confidence": 0.63, "feedback": "Hydroxyacetone-to-lactaldehyde isomerization is structurally possible through enediol chemistry, but useful lactaldehyde availability is not established. The ketone-aldehyde equilibrium, reaction rate and selective trapping must be quantified. Unconverted hydroxyacetone can undergo competing cyanohydrin or Strecker chemistry, producing a different carbon connectivity. Once lactaldehyde is obtained, the proposed target route is coherent, subject to the unspecified hydrolysis conditions.", "approved": false, "problematic_reactions": ["rxn_003", "rxn_007", "rxn_008"], "suggestions": ["Measure the hydroxyacetone/lactaldehyde equilibrium and interconversion kinetics under explicitly stated surface conditions.", "Test whether cyanide trapping captures lactaldehyde selectively enough to outcompete direct hydroxyacetone reactions.", "Specify staged hydrolysis conditions and confirm preservation of the beta-hydroxyl group."]}, {"pathway_id": "P6", "reaction_scores": {"rxn_003": 0.55, "rxn_006": 0.86, "rxn_007": 0.75, "rxn_008": 0.73}, "chemical_feasibility": 0.7, "pathway_coherence": 0.85, "environmental_progression": 0.61, "target_achievement": 0.72, "overall_confidence": 0.65, "feedback": "The lactaldehyde-to-threonine portion is a valid generic Strecker strategy. Its upstream connection remains conditional because rxn_003 has no specified catalyst, pH, equilibrium composition or demonstrated productive flux. Direct Strecker reaction of residual hydroxyacetone would give a constitutional isomer rather than threonine. Final hydrolysis is chemically plausible but cannot be assumed efficient in unspecified surface water.", "approved": false, "problematic_reactions": ["rxn_003", "rxn_007", "rxn_008"], "suggestions": ["Identify an environmentally available acid/base or other catalytic regime for rxn_003 and establish its selectivity.", "Quantify lactaldehyde capture relative to ketone Strecker chemistry, explicitly distinguishing threonine from alpha-methylserine.", "Demonstrate a hydrolysis window that gives measurable threonine accumulation rather than only aminonitrile or amide formation."]}, {"pathway_id": "P7", "reaction_scores": {"rxn_003": 0.55, "rxn_004": 0.91, "rxn_005": 0.82, "rxn_009": 0.65, "rxn_010": 0.58}, "chemical_feasibility": 0.62, "pathway_coherence": 0.79, "environmental_progression": 0.46, "target_achievement": 0.59, "overall_confidence": 0.55, "feedback": "The formal sequence is connected and carbon-consistent, but productive operation depends on three unresolved transformations: selective lactaldehyde supply, hydantoin trapping and hydrothermal release of surviving threonine. Hydantoin formation could provide a useful intermediate reservoir, but that protective function has not been demonstrated for this substrate. Environmental labels do not establish the needed reaction conditions or justify transport between settings.", "approved": false, "problematic_reactions": ["rxn_003", "rxn_009", "rxn_010"], "suggestions": ["Verify lactaldehyde supply and structurally identify 5-(1-hydroxyethyl)hydantoin, excluding isomeric products derived from hydroxyacetone.", "Measure hydantoin formation, storage stability and hydrolysis under a consistent environmental scenario.", "Specify transport, residence time and product-preservation conditions for the hydrothermal stage."]}, {"pathway_id": "P8", "reaction_scores": {"rxn_003": 0.55, "rxn_006": 0.86, "rxn_009": 0.65, "rxn_010": 0.58}, "chemical_feasibility": 0.64, "pathway_coherence": 0.81, "environmental_progression": 0.47, "target_achievement": 0.6, "overall_confidence": 0.57, "feedback": "Direct Strecker assembly is plausible if lactaldehyde is available, and the hydantoin route preserves the intended amino-acid skeleton. Nevertheless, rxn_003 has not established sufficient selective precursor supply, while rxn_009 and rxn_010 lack substrate-specific operating evidence. The principal environmental issue is not the use of two environments but the absence of justified transfer and conditions that favor product formation over destruction.", "approved": false, "problematic_reactions": ["rxn_003", "rxn_009", "rxn_010"], "suggestions": ["Demonstrate selective lactaldehyde production and capture in the presence of hydroxyacetone.", "Determine carbon dioxide trapping and hydantoin hydrolysis kinetics for the hydroxyl-substituted substrate.", "Provide a physical transfer scenario and show that threonine survives the hydrothermal residence time."]}, {"pathway_id": "P9", "reaction_scores": {"rxn_011": 0.93, "rxn_012": 0.86, "rxn_014": 0.79, "rxn_015": 0.3}, "chemical_feasibility": 0.46, "pathway_coherence": 0.65, "environmental_progression": 0.5, "target_achievement": 0.35, "overall_confidence": 0.41, "feedback": "The glycine-producing branch uses credible generic chemistry, although hydrolysis conditions remain unspecified. Glycine plus acetaldehyde has the correct carbon connectivity for a threonine aldol addition, unlike the review's alanine-formaldehyde assignment. However, rxn_015 lacks a demonstrated means of activating glycine's alpha-carbon. Imine formation, acetaldehyde self-reaction and retro-aldol chemistry can compete. A balanced equation does not establish useful target production.", "approved": false, "problematic_reactions": ["rxn_014", "rxn_015"], "suggestions": ["Specify a chemically credible, environmentally available activation or catalytic system for glycine alpha-carbon addition.", "Measure threonine formation against competing reactions and the reverse aldol process.", "Provide direct evidence for the glycine-acetaldehyde substrate pair rather than treating the review's different, incorrectly assigned reaction as experimental support."]}, {"pathway_id": "P10", "reaction_scores": {"rxn_013": 0.9, "rxn_014": 0.79, "rxn_015": 0.3}, "chemical_feasibility": 0.47, "pathway_coherence": 0.66, "environmental_progression": 0.5, "target_achievement": 0.35, "overall_confidence": 0.42, "feedback": "Direct formaldehyde Strecker synthesis supplies a credible route to glycine, subject to appropriate hydrolysis conditions. The target-forming step remains the dominant weakness: unactivated glycine is not an efficient carbon nucleophile under generic aqueous surface conditions. The proposed aldol addition is structurally sensible but experimentally and environmentally under-specified. Improving glycine supply alone would not validate threonine production through this branch.", "approved": false, "problematic_reactions": ["rxn_014", "rxn_015"], "suggestions": ["Define and test the acid/base, metal-assisted or other activation mechanism intended for rxn_015 without assuming an unspecified catalyst.", "Report product identity, yield, threonine/allo-threonine distribution and competing acetaldehyde reactions.", "Specify aminoacetonitrile hydrolysis conditions and whether staging is needed before the aldol experiment."]}], "best_pathway_id": "P2", "overall_confidence": 0.66, "network_feedback": "Not approved: overall confidence is below the 0.90 network threshold. Formal network validity is high: the 19 molecule nodes and 15 reaction nodes are connected, identifiers are consistent, and the deterministic check establishes that every reaction conserves atoms and charge with suitable coefficients. Explicitly include the supplied coefficients for rxn_002, rxn_010 and rxn_014. The coefficient eight in rxn_002 follows from representing elemental sulfur as S8 and is not an implausible elementary molecularity requirement. Flagged reactions have evidential, kinetic or environmental issues, not conservation violations. The corrected C4 lactaldehyde cyanohydrin, imine-based interpretation of Strecker entry and exclusion of the review's incorrectly mapped alanine-formaldehyde route are chemically justified. Nevertheless, P1-P8 depend on insufficiently established lactaldehyde supply through rxn_002 or rxn_003, followed by processing whose effective conditions and product survival are unspecified. P9-P10 share the substantially weaker glycine-acetaldehyde target-forming step. Anoxia is compatible with the proposal, but Surface and Hydrothermal labels do not establish pH, temperature, pressure, irradiation, catalytic availability or residence time; hydantoin transfer also needs physical justification. Literature alignment is partial: established reaction families support the general strategy, and the network appropriately acknowledges speculation and review errors, but reaction-specific citations and experimental protocols are absent. The supplied review's claims attributed to Patel et al. (2015) and Aubrey et al. (2008) should be checked against primary experimental records rather than accepted as proof of these exact corrected sequences. Unresolved stereochemistry is not penalized because the target is explicitly connectivity-defined. Confidence reflects the connected network's chemical bottlenecks, not