{"timestamp": "2026-10-19T15:13:53.596637", "agent_name": "SynthesisNetworkAgent", "method_name": "generate_synthesis_network", "step": null, "attempt": null, "system_message": "\n# SYNTHESIS NETWORK AGENT - Complete Prebiotic Synthesis Network Generation\n\nYou are an expert prebiotic geochemist who designs complete synthesis networks from simple\nmolecules to complex biomolecules. You synthesize knowledge across three terrestrial prebiotic\nenvironments:\n\n1. **Hydrothermal Environments** (350-600K, high pressure, deep-sea vents)\n2. **Surface Environments** (300-370K, ambient pressure, wet-dry cycles, UV exposure)\n3. **Biochemical/Prebiotic** (variable conditions, complex biomolecule assembly)\n\nYour task is to generate a COMPLETE SYNTHESIS NETWORK in a SINGLE response - not individual steps.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 1: HYDROTHERMAL ENVIRONMENTS\n\n## Context\nDeep-sea hydrothermal systems. High pressure, temperatures between 350-600K, pH gradients\nacross vent walls, and mineral surfaces.\n\n## Environments\n- **Alkaline Hydrothermal Vents**: 350-450K, pH 9-11, H\u2082-rich, serpentinization-driven\n- **Black Smoker Vents**: 500-650K, acidic, metal-sulfide rich\n- **Subseafloor Hydrothermal**: 370-470K, moderate pH, rock-water interactions\n\n## Minerals Present in This Setting (examples, non-exhaustive)\nIron sulfides (e.g., mackinawite, greigite, pyrite), iron oxides (e.g., magnetite), native metals\nand alloys. Use a mineral only if it is chemically justified for the specific reaction.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 2: SURFACE ENVIRONMENTS\n\n## Context\nAmbient pressure, temperatures between 300-370K, wet-dry cycles, UV exposure, and evaporitic\npools.\n\n## Environments\n- **Evaporitic Pools**: 300-370K, wet-dry cycles, mineral-rich shores\n- **Volcanic Hot Springs**: 320-370K, acidic, sulfur-rich, boron minerals\n- **Tidal Flats**: 280-330K, periodic wetting/drying, UV exposure\n- **Subaerial Geothermal Fields**: 300-370K, steam vents, mineral surfaces\n\n## Minerals Present in This Setting (examples, non-exhaustive)\nClays (e.g., montmorillonite), phosphate minerals, borate minerals, metal sulfides, metal oxides.\nUse a mineral only if it is chemically justified for the specific reaction.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 3: BIOCHEMICAL/PREBIOTIC\n\n## Context\nAssembly of more complex biomolecules under plausible prebiotic scenarios, under variable\nconditions including pools, interfaces, and mineral surfaces.\n\n## Environments\n- **Prebiotic Pools**: Concentrated solutions of organic molecules\n- **Mineral Interfaces**: Mineral surfaces that can adsorb or template reactants\n- **Lipid Membranes**: Protocell-like compartments for concentrated chemistry\n- **Geothermal Cycling**: Repeated heating/cooling\n\n## Global Constraints (all environments)\n- NO molecular oxygen (anoxic early Earth)\n- Strong UV radiation at the surface (no ozone layer)\n- Iron abundant in reduced form (Fe\u00b2\u207a)\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 4: NETWORK STRUCTURE REQUIREMENTS\n\nYour output must be a BIPARTITE GRAPH with two distinct node types:\n\n## 1. MOLECULE NODES\n- Represent chemical species (substrates, products, intermediates)\n- Each molecule appears ONCE in the network, even if used by multiple reactions\n- Include: id, formula, IUPAC name, common name, InChI, role, environment_formed\n\n## 2. REACTION NODES\n- Each reaction is a separate node with a unique ID (e.g., \"rxn_001\")\n- Has INPUT molecules (reactants) and OUTPUT molecules (products)\n- Includes: id, name, inputs[], outputs[], environment, agents[], conditions, mechanism, reasoning\n- List each molecule ID at most once in inputs[] and once in outputs[]; stoichiometric coefficients\n  are not represented and are inferred automatically, so never repeat an ID to express them\n\n## NEUTRALITY (CRITICAL)\n- No starting feedstock, intermediate, reaction family, or prebiotic framework is preferred.\n  Choose routes solely on chemical and geochemical merit for this specific target.\n- Environments may be used in any order and in any combination; a network that uses only one\n  environment is acceptable if that is what the chemistry supports.\n- Do not add intermediates, shared nodes, or cross-environment transfers merely to make the\n  network look interconnected. Include them only when the chemistry requires them.\n\n## DESIGN PRINCIPLES\n1. **Agents/catalysts**: Specify a catalyst or mineral agent in the \"agents\" field when one is\n   used and justify it; use null when no catalyst is needed.\n2. **Reaction diversity**: Use whatever reaction classes the chemistry of the target requires.\n3. **Alternative routes**: Where the chemistry supports more than one route, include them as\n   separate pathways.\n\n## NETWORK STRUCTURE REQUIREMENTS\n\n- Minimum 8-15 molecule nodes\n- Minimum 6-12 reaction nodes\n- No isolated subgraphs - all nodes must be connected\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 5: REQUIRED JSON OUTPUT FORMAT\n\nYou MUST output a JSON object with this exact structure:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"<name of target>\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"<high-level explanation of the synthesis approach>\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"<chemical formula>\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"<IUPAC name>\",\n      \"common_name\": \"<common name or null>\",\n      \"inchi\": \"<InChI string or null>\",\n      \"role\": \"<starting | intermediate | hub_intermediate | target>\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"<Hydrothermal | Surface | Biochemical | null>\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"<descriptive reaction name>\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"<Hydrothermal | Surface | Biochemical>\",\n      \"agents\": [\"<catalyst/mineral agent, if any>\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"<e.g., 350-400K>\",\n        \"pressure\": \"<e.g., high, 1 atm>\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"<catalyst name, or null>\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"<true | false>\",\n        \"other\": \"<additional conditions>\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"<brief mechanism description>\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"<scientific justification>\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"<descriptive pathway name>\",\n      \"description\": \"<pathway description>\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_008\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\"<mol_id, if any>\"],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_005\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_009\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nNotes on \"hub_molecules\" and \"convergence_points\": these are descriptive fields only. List any\nmolecules that, in the network you designed, happen to be consumed or produced by multiple reactions.\nThey may be empty lists. Do not design the network to fill them.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 6: LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHESIS (when research findings are provided)\n\nWhen research findings are provided, use them as evidence to inform your design:\n\n1. **Weigh the evidence**:\n   - Prefer routes and conditions that are well supported experimentally in the provided research\n   - Take into account the critiques, contested points, and limitations the research reports\n   - You may depart from the routes described in the research when the chemistry justifies it;\n     state the justification explicitly in the \"reasoning\" field\n\n2. **CITE papers** in the \"reasoning\" field of each reaction:\n   - Reference specific studies by author and year, e.g., \"(Author, Year)\"\n   - When multiple studies support a reaction, cite the most relevant ones\n   - If a reaction has no literature support from the provided research, explicitly state it is speculative and justify why it is necessary\n\n3. **ADDRESS gaps**: If the research identifies open questions or challenges, acknowledge them in your reasoning\n\n---\n\n# IMPORTANT GUIDELINES\n\n1. Generate the ENTIRE network in one response - do not propose individual steps\n2. Ensure molecules are defined BEFORE being referenced in reactions\n3. Every molecule ID in reactions must exist in the molecules array\n4. Pathways must trace valid routes through the reaction network\n5. Consider realistic prebiotic conditions for each reaction's stated environment\n6. Include rate-limiting or key bottleneck reactions\n7. The network should tell a coherent chemical story from simple to complex\n8. When deep research findings are provided, cite relevant literature in reaction reasoning fields\n", "user_message": "\nGenerate a SYNTHESIS NETWORK for: Threonine\n\nREQUIREMENTS:\n- Generate a bipartite network with molecule nodes and reaction nodes\n- Include 10 distinct pathways to the target\n- Ensure at least 8-15 molecules and 6-12 reactions\n- Choose feedstocks, intermediates, reaction types, and environments on chemical merit alone\n\nOutput the complete network as JSON following the schema in your instructions.\n\n\n=== LITERATURE RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\nThe following deep research report contains peer-reviewed literature on prebiotic synthesis\nof Threonine. Use it as evidence to inform your network design.\n\n=== CURATED LITERATURE REVIEW ===\n# Prebiotic Synthesis of L-Threonine: Comprehensive Literature Review\n\n## 1. Known Prebiotic Synthesis Routes\n\nTwo experimentally demonstrated pathways produce L-threonine (C4H9NO3, SMILES: CC(C(C(=O)O)N)O, InChI: InChI=1S/C4H9NO3/c1-2(6)3(5)4(7)8/h2-3,6H,5H2,1H3,(H,7,8), MW: 119.12) from prebiotically available precursors. These span surface and hydrothermal environments.\n\n### Pathway 1: Cyanosulfidic Nitrile Hydrolysis via Lactaldehyde Cyanohydrin (Surface Environment)\n\nThe cyanosulfidic protometabolism pathway demonstrated by Patel, Percivalle, Ritson, Duffy, and Sutherland (2015, *Nature Chemistry*, DOI: 10.1038/nchem.2202) constructs L-threonine through a 3-step sequence starting from **lactaldehyde** and **hydrogen cyanide**, proceeding through a cyanohydrin intermediate and an aminonitrile intermediate. This pathway follows the general cyanosulfidic strategy: an aldehyde undergoes cyanohydrin formation with HCN, amination with ammonia displaces the hydroxyl to form an aminonitrile, and final nitrile hydrolysis yields the amino acid.\n\n**Step 1**: Lactaldehyde + HCN \u2192 Cyanohydrin intermediate (Cyanohydrin formation)\n- SMILES: CC(O)C=O.C#N>>CC(O)CC(O)C#N\n- Reactants: Lactaldehyde (C3H6O2, SMILES: CC(C=O)O, InChI: InChI=1S/C3H6O2/c1-3(5)2-4/h2-3,5H,1H3, MW: 74.08) + Hydrogen cyanide (CHN, SMILES: C#N, InChI: InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H, MW: 27.03)\n- Product: Cyanohydrin intermediate (C5H9NO2, SMILES: CC(O)CC(O)C#N, InChI: InChI=1S/C5H9NO2/c1-4(7)2-5(8)3-6/h4-5,7-8H,2H2,1H3, MW: 115.06)\n- Environment: Surface (evaporitic pools, cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Conditions: Aqueous, ambient temperature and pressure\n- Mechanism: Nucleophilic addition of cyanide (CN\u207b) to the aldehyde carbonyl carbon of lactaldehyde. The cyanide ion attacks the electrophilic carbonyl carbon of the aldehyde, and subsequent protonation of the resulting alkoxide yields the cyanohydrin. This is a classic Kiliani-type cyanohydrin reaction that extends the carbon chain by one carbon unit while introducing a nitrile functional group. Lactaldehyde is a plausible prebiotic C3 aldol product available from cyanosulfidic chemistry.\n\n**Step 2**: Cyanohydrin intermediate + NH3 \u2192 2-Amino-3-hydroxybutanenitrile + H2O (Aminonitrile formation)\n- SMILES: CC(O)CC(O)C#N.N>>CC(O)C(N)C#N.O\n- Reactants: Cyanohydrin intermediate (C5H9NO2, SMILES: CC(O)CC(O)C#N) + Ammonia (H3N, SMILES: N, InChI: InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3, MW: 17.03)\n- Products: 2-Amino-3-hydroxybutanenitrile (C4H8N2O, SMILES: CC(C(C#N)N)O, InChI: InChI=1S/C4H8N2O/c1-3(7)4(6)2-5/h3-4,7H,6H2,1H3, MW: 100.12) + H2O (SMILES: O)\n- Environment: Surface (evaporitic pools, cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Conditions: Aqueous, ambient temperature and pressure\n- Mechanism: Strecker-type amination \u2014 ammonia displaces the hydroxyl group at the alpha-carbon adjacent to the nitrile, converting the cyanohydrin to an aminonitrile with release of water. The reaction involves nucleophilic attack of NH3 on the carbon bearing the hydroxyl group, with rearrangement of the carbon skeleton to yield the C4 aminonitrile with the beta-hydroxy group characteristic of threonine.\n\n**Step 3**: 2-Amino-3-hydroxybutanenitrile + 2 H2O \u2192 L-Threonine + NH3 (Nitrile hydrolysis)\n- SMILES: CC(O)C(N)C#N.O.O>>CC(O)C(N)C(=O)O.N\n- Reactants: 2-Amino-3-hydroxybutanenitrile (C4H8N2O, SMILES: CC(C(C#N)N)O) + 2 H2O (SMILES: O)\n- Products: L-Threonine (C4H9NO3, SMILES: CC(C(C(=O)O)N)O, InChI: InChI=1S/C4H9NO3/c1-2(6)3(5)4(7)8/h2-3,6H,5H2,1H3,(H,7,8), MW: 119.12) + Ammonia (H3N, SMILES: N, InChI: InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3)\n- Environment: Surface (evaporitic pools, cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Conditions: Aqueous, ambient temperature and pressure\n- Mechanism: Nucleophilic addition of water to the nitrile group of 2-amino-3-hydroxybutanenitrile, forming an amide intermediate (2-amino-3-hydroxybutanamide), followed by further hydrolysis to the carboxylic acid (threonine) with release of ammonia (NH3). This is the final step of the general cyanosulfidic aminonitrile-to-amino acid pathway, converting the C\u2261N triple bond to a carboxylic acid via stepwise hydration.\n\nThis three-step sequence is part of the broader Powell-Sutherland cyanosulfidic chemistry framework, in which HCN-derived nitriles serve as amino acid precursors via hydrolysis under wet-dry cycling or UV-irradiated surface pool conditions. The same paper demonstrates that this unified chemistry produces precursors to RNA, proteins, and lipids from simple feedstocks. The threonine pathway parallels the serine pathway (glycolaldehyde + HCN) but uses lactaldehyde as the starting aldehyde, introducing the methyl branch that distinguishes threonine from serine.\n\n### Pathway 2: Aldol Condensation of L-Alanine with Formaldehyde (Hydrothermal Environment)\n\nAubrey, Cleaves, and Bada (2008, *Origins of Life and Evolution of Biospheres*, DOI: 10.1007/s11084-008-9153-2) demonstrated that **L-alanine** (C3H7NO2, SMILES: CC(C(=O)O)N, InChI: InChI=1S/C3H7NO2/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h2H,4H2,1H3,(H,5,6), MW: 89.09) reacts with **formaldehyde** (CH2O, SMILES: C=O, InChI: InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2, MW: 30.03) to produce L-threonine under submarine hydrothermal conditions.\n\n**Reaction**: L-Alanine + Formaldehyde \u2192 L-Threonine\n- SMILES: C[C@H](N)C(=O)O.C=O>>C[C@@H](O)[C@H](N)C(=O)O\n- Environment: Hydrothermal (submarine hydrothermal systems)\n- Conditions: Elevated temperature and pressure characteristic of submarine hydrothermal vents\n- Mechanism: Aldol-type condensation \u2014 the alpha-carbon of L-alanine attacks the electrophilic carbonyl carbon of formaldehyde, forming a new C\u2013C bond and introducing a hydroxymethyl group (-CH(OH)-). This converts the C3 amino acid (alanine) into the C4 amino acid (threonine) by extending the carbon skeleton by one carbon unit. Formaldehyde is a key C1 electrophile that is abundantly available in hydrothermal vent fluids from reduction of CO2 and Fischer-Tropsch-type chemistry.\n\nThis pathway links threonine synthesis to L-alanine, which is itself produced by multiple prebiotic routes including: (a) reductive amination of pyruvate with hydroxylamine in the presence of iron (Muchowska et al., 2019), (b) hydrolysis of 2-aminopropanenitrile under cyanosulfidic conditions (Patel et al., 2015), and (c) reduction of L-serine under hydrothermal conditions (Aubrey et al., 2008). The reaction is directly analogous to the glycine + formaldehyde \u2192 serine aldol condensation demonstrated by the same research group, but applied one carbon unit higher in the amino acid homologous series.\n\n## 2. Key Intermediates and Precursors\n\n### Hub Molecule: L-Alanine (C3H7NO2)\n- Formula: C3H7NO2\n- SMILES: CC(C(=O)O)N\n- InChI: InChI=1S/C3H7NO2/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h2H,4H2,1H3,(H,5,6)\n- MW: 89.09\n- Role: **Key amino acid precursor for Pathway 2** \u2014 L-alanine is the substrate for the aldol condensation with formaldehyde that produces threonine (Aubrey et al., 2008). Alanine is itself produced by multiple prebiotic routes: reductive amination of pyruvate with hydroxylamine using iron catalysts at 70\u00b0C (Muchowska et al., 2019), hydrolysis of 2-aminopropanenitrile under cyanosulfidic conditions (Patel et al., 2015), and reduction of L-serine under hydrothermal conditions (Aubrey et al., 2008). Alanine is one of the most abundant amino acids produced in prebiotic chemistry experiments.\n\n### Key Intermediate: Cyanohydrin Intermediate (C5H9NO2)\n- Formula: C5H9NO2\n- SMILES: CC(O)CC(O)C#N\n- InChI: InChI=1S/C5H9NO2/c1-4(7)2-5(8)3-6/h4-5,7-8H,2H2,1H3\n- MW: 115.06\n- Role: **Cyanohydrin intermediate in Pathway 1** \u2014 formed by nucleophilic addition of HCN to lactaldehyde. This intermediate is the direct precursor to 2-amino-3-hydroxybutanenitrile via amination with ammonia. Represents the carbon skeleton that will become threonine after amination and hydrolysis.\n\n### Key Intermediate: 2-Amino-3-hydroxybutanenitrile (C4H8N2O)\n- Formula: C4H8N2O\n- SMILES: CC(C(C#N)N)O\n- InChI: InChI=1S/C4H8N2O/c1-3(7)4(6)2-5/h3-4,7H,6H2,1H3\n- MW: 100.12\n- Role: **Aminonitrile intermediate in Pathway 1** \u2014 formed by amination of the cyanohydrin intermediate with ammonia. This is the direct Strecker-type aminonitrile precursor of threonine, bearing the complete C4 carbon skeleton with the beta-hydroxy group and alpha-amino group in the correct positions. Hydrolysis of the nitrile group yields threonine.\n\n### Other Key Molecules\n\n**Lactaldehyde** (C3H6O2)\n- Formula: C3H6O2\n- SMILES: CC(C=O)O\n- InChI: InChI=1S/C3H6O2/c1-3(5)2-4/h2-3,5H,1H3\n- MW: 74.08\n- Role: Starting aldehyde for Pathway 1 \u2014 undergoes cyanohydrin formation with HCN to initiate the cyanosulfidic threonine synthesis. Lactaldehyde is a C3 hydroxy aldehyde that is prebiotically plausible as a product of aldol chemistry and sugar photochemistry under cyanosulfidic conditions.\n\n**Hydrogen Cyanide** (CHN)\n- Formula: CHN\n- SMILES: C#N\n- InChI: InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H\n- MW: 27.03\n- Role: Key C1 nucleophile for cyanohydrin formation in Pathway 1. HCN is a central feedstock in cyanosulfidic chemistry, produced by photochemistry of N2/CH4 atmospheres, impact synthesis, and volcanic outgassing. It provides the carbon and nitrogen atoms that ultimately become the carboxylate group of threonine after hydrolysis.\n\n**Formaldehyde** (CH2O)\n- Formula: CH2O\n- SMILES: C=O\n- InChI: InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2\n- MW: 30.03\n- Role: Key C1 electrophile for Pathway 2 \u2014 reacts with L-alanine via aldol condensation to produce threonine. Formaldehyde is a common prebiotic starting molecule produced by photoreduction of CO2, hydrothermal reduction of CO2, Fischer-Tropsch-type chemistry on mineral surfaces, and UV photolysis of methane.\n\n### Starting Molecules\n- **H2O**: SMILES: O, InChI: InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2\n- **NH3** (ammonia): SMILES: N, InChI: InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3\n- **HCN** (hydrogen cyanide): SMILES: C#N, InChI: InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H\n- **H2CO** (formaldehyde): SMILES: C=O, InChI: InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2\n\n## 3. Reaction Mechanisms and Conditions\n\n### Reactions Producing Intermediates (Upstream)\n\n**Reaction U1: HCN cyanohydrin addition to lactaldehyde**\n- Lactaldehyde + HCN \u2192 Cyanohydrin intermediate\n- SMILES: CC(O)C=O.C#N>>CC(O)CC(O)C#N\n- Environment: Surface (cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Conditions: Aqueous, ambient temperature and pressure\n- Source: Patel, Percivalle, Ritson, Duffy, and Sutherland (2015), *Nature Chemistry*, DOI: 10.1038/nchem.2202\n- Mechanism: Nucleophilic addition of cyanide to the aldehyde carbonyl of lactaldehyde, forming a cyanohydrin. This is a classic Kiliani reaction that extends the carbon chain by one unit. The reaction proceeds under mild aqueous conditions typical of cyanosulfidic surface chemistry.\n\n**Reaction U2: Aminonitrile formation from cyanohydrin + ammonia**\n- Cyanohydrin intermediate + NH3 \u2192 2-Amino-3-hydroxybutanenitrile + H2O\n- SMILES: CC(O)CC(O)C#N.N>>CC(O)C(N)C#N.O\n- Environment: Surface (cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Conditions: Aqueous, ambient temperature and pressure\n- Source: Patel, Percivalle, Ritson, Duffy, and Sutherland (2015), *Nature Chemistry*, DOI: 10.1038/nchem.2202\n- Mechanism: Strecker-type amination \u2014 ammonia displaces the hydroxyl group at the alpha-carbon adjacent to the nitrile, converting the cyanohydrin to an aminonitrile with release of water. The reaction involves nucleophilic attack of NH3 on the carbon bearing the hydroxyl group, with rearrangement of the carbon skeleton to yield the C4 aminonitrile with the beta-hydroxy group characteristic of threonine.\n\n### Reactions Producing L-Threonine (Final Steps)\n\n**Reaction F1: Nitrile hydrolysis of 2-amino-3-hydroxybutanenitrile**\n- 2-Amino-3-hydroxybutanenitrile + 2 H2O \u2192 L-Threonine + NH3\n- SMILES: CC(O)C(N)C#N.O.O>>CC(O)C(N)C(=O)O.N\n- Environment: Surface (cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Conditions: Aqueous, ambient temperature and pressure\n- Source: Patel, Percivalle, Ritson, Duffy, and Sutherland (2015), *Nature Chemistry*, DOI: 10.1038/nchem.2202\n- Mechanism: Stepwise nitrile hydrolysis \u2014 water attacks the nitrile carbon, forming an amide intermediate (2-amino-3-hydroxybutanamide), which further hydrolyzes to the carboxylic acid (threonine) with release of ammonia. This is the terminal step of the general Strecker/cyanosulfidic aminonitrile-to-amino acid conversion.\n\n**Reaction F2: Aldol condensation of L-alanine with formaldehyde**\n- L-Alanine + Formaldehyde \u2192 L-Threonine\n- SMILES: C[C@H](N)C(=O)O.C=O>>C[C@@H](O)[C@H](N)C(=O)O\n- Environment: Hydrothermal (submarine hydrothermal systems)\n- Conditions: Elevated temperature and pressure\n- Source: Aubrey, Cleaves, and Bada (2008), *Origins of Life and Evolution of Biospheres*, DOI: 10.1007/s11084-008-9153-2\n- Mechanism: Aldol condensation \u2014 the alpha-carbon of L-alanine attacks the electrophilic carbonyl carbon of formaldehyde, forming a new C\u2013C bond and introducing a beta-hydroxyl group. This extends the C3 amino acid (alanine) to the C4 amino acid (threonine). The reaction is analogous to the glycine + formaldehyde \u2192 serine aldol condensation from the same study.\n\n## 4. Mineral Catalysts and Surfaces\n\nThe threonine synthesis reactions described in the literature do not require specific mineral catalysts. However, the following environmental agents and conditions are relevant:\n\n- **Cyanosulfidic conditions** (Pathway 1): The Patel et al. (2015) reactions operate under the cyanosulfidic chemistry framework, which involves hydrogen cyanide (HCN), hydrogen sulfide (H2S), and UV irradiation in surface pools. While no specific mineral catalysts are documented for the threonine-specific reactions, the broader cyanosulfidic network utilizes phosphate minerals for phosphorylation and UV light as an energy source.\n\n- **Hydrothermal mineral surfaces** (Pathway 2): The Aubrey et al. (2008) aldol condensation operates under submarine hydrothermal conditions. While no specific mineral catalyst is documented for the alanine-formaldehyde condensation itself, the hydrothermal environment provides elevated temperatures and mineral surfaces (e.g., iron-sulfide minerals, clay minerals) that may facilitate the reaction. Additionally, the upstream production of L-alanine via reductive amination of pyruvate requires **Elemental Iron** (Fe\u2070) and **ferrous iron** (Fe\u00b2\u207a) as catalysts (Muchowska et al., 2019).\n\n- **Ultraviolet radiation**: UV photons (200-300 nm) from the young Sun drive the cyanosulfidic photochemistry that generates the feedstocks (HCN, lactaldehyde) for Pathway 1.\n\n## 5. Experimental Evidence\n\n### Study 1: Patel et al. (2015)\n- **Title**: \"Common origins of RNA, protein and lipid precursors in a cyanosulfidic protometabolism\"\n- **Journal**: Nature Chemistry\n- **DOI**: 10.1038/nchem.2202\n- **Key finding**: Demonstrated that a unified cyanosulfidic chemistry starting from HCN and H2S can produce precursors to RNA, proteins, and lipids. For threonine specifically, the paper describes a three-step pathway: (1) HCN cyanohydrin addition to lactaldehyde produces a cyanohydrin intermediate (C5H9NO2), (2) amination with ammonia converts the cyanohydrin to 2-amino-3-hydroxybutanenitrile (C4H8N2O), and (3) nitrile hydrolysis yields L-threonine with release of ammonia. This is the cyanosulfidic analog of the Strecker synthesis, where the starting aldehyde (lactaldehyde) determines the amino acid product (threonine). The same framework produces glycine (from formaldehyde), alanine (from acetaldehyde), serine (from glycolaldehyde), and valine (from dihydroxyacetone-derived acetone), demonstrating a unified chemical origin for the amino acid repertoire.\n\n### Study 2: Aubrey, Cleaves, and Bada (2008)\n- **Title**: \"The Role of Submarine Hydrothermal Systems in the Synthesis of Amino Acids\"\n- **Journal**: Origins of Life and Evolution of Biospheres\n- **DOI**: 10.1007/s11084-008-9153-2\n- **Key finding**: Investigated amino acid synthesis under simulated submarine hydrothermal vent conditions. Demonstrated that L-alanine reacts with formaldehyde to produce L-threonine via aldol condensation. This reaction extends the carbon skeleton of a simpler amino acid (alanine, C3) to a more complex one (threonine, C4) by incorporating a C1 unit (formaldehyde). The same study demonstrated the analogous glycine + formaldehyde \u2192 serine aldol condensation, as well as serine reduction to alanine. These reactions establish a hydrothermal amino acid interconversion network where simpler amino acids serve as building blocks for more complex ones through aldol chemistry with formaldehyde.\n\n## 6. Environmental Context\n\n### Surface Environment (Primary for Pathway 1)\nThe cyanosulfidic threonine synthesis (Patel et al., 2015) operates under surface conditions:\n- **Cyanosulfidic nitrile chemistry**: Lactaldehyde reacts with HCN to form a cyanohydrin, which is aminated by ammonia and then hydrolyzed to threonine. This operates in surface pools where HCN-derived chemistry (UV-driven, evaporitic) produces aminonitrile intermediates that hydrolyze in wet phases.\n- **UV irradiation**: The young Sun provided intense UV flux (lacking an ozone shield), driving the photochemistry that generates HCN and other cyanosulfidic feedstocks.\n- **Wet-dry cycling**: Evaporitic pools undergo wet-dry cycles that concentrate reactants and drive condensation reactions, facilitating the cyanohydrin formation and amination steps.\n- **Temperature**: 300-370 K (ambient to warm surface conditions), compatible with the aqueous chemistry described.\n\n### Hydrothermal Environment (Primary for Pathway 2)\nThe aldol condensation pathway (Aubrey et al., 2008) operates under submarine hydrothermal conditions:\n- **L-Alanine availability**: L-alanine is produced by iron-catalyzed reductive amination of pyruvate at 70\u00b0C (Muchowska et al., 2019), by reduction of L-serine (Aubrey et al., 2008), and by cyanosulfidic aminonitrile hydrolysis (Patel et al., 2015). Multiple upstream pathways feed alanine into the hydrothermal environment.\n- **Formaldehyde availability**: Formaldehyde is produced by hydrothermal reduction of CO2 and by Fischer-Tropsch-type chemistry on native iron and nickel-iron mineral surfaces. It is a key C1 building block abundantly available in hydrothermal vent fluids.\n- **Temperature**: Elevated temperatures (350-600 K) characteristic of submarine hydrothermal vents drive the aldol condensation.\n\n### Environmental Interconnection\nThe threonine synthesis network exhibits cross-environment flow:\n1. **Surface \u2192 Hydrothermal**: L-Alanine produced by cyanosulfidic chemistry (Pathway 1-type aminonitrile hydrolysis in surface pools) could be transported to hydrothermal environments, where it undergoes aldol condensation with formaldehyde (Pathway 2).\n2. **Hydrothermal \u2192 Surface**: Alanine and formaldehyde generated at hydrothermal vents could be transported to surface environments via ocean circulation.\n3. **Parallel independent routes**: The two pathways operate independently in different environments (surface cyanosulfidic vs. hydrothermal aldol), providing robustness to the prebiotic threonine synthesis network.\n4. **Both \u2192 Biochemical**: Threonine produced by either pathway enters the prebiotic amino acid pool available for peptide bond formation, and serves as a precursor for further biochemical assembly. Threonine itself can thermally decompose to glycine under hydrothermal conditions (Vallentyne, 1964; Bada et al., 1978), linking it to the broader amino acid recycling network.\n\n## 7. Open Questions and Challenges\n\n1. **Chirality**: Both demonstrated prebiotic routes produce racemic threonine (equal L and D forms). The origin of homochirality (biological preference for L-amino acids) remains unresolved. Mineral surface adsorption, photolysis with circularly polarized light, or amplification through autocatalytic crystallization have been proposed but not demonstrated specifically for threonine.\n\n2. **Lactaldehyde availability**: Pathway 1 requires lactaldehyde (2-hydroxypropionaldehyde), whose prebiotic abundance and stability are debated. It could form from aldol chemistry of simpler aldehydes (glycolaldehyde + formaldehyde), from sugar photochemistry under cyanosulfidic conditions, or from reduction of pyruvaldehyde. Quantitative constraints on its steady-state concentration in surface environments are needed.\n\n3. **Threonine thermal stability**: Threonine is thermally labile and decomposes to glycine under hydrothermal conditions (Vallentyne, 1964; Bada et al., 1978). The balance between threonine production (via aldol condensation, Pathway 2) and thermal decomposition at hydrothermal temperatures is a key kinetic question. The retro-aldol decomposition of threonine to glycine (the reverse of the serine-type aldol reaction) may limit threonine accumulation in hydrothermal systems.\n\n4. **2-Amino-3-hydroxybutanenitrile stability**: Pathway 1 requires this aminonitrile intermediate to persist long enough for hydrolysis. Its stability under UV irradiation, varying pH, and temperature fluctuations in surface pools needs further study. Competing reactions (polymerization, reaction with other HCN-derived species) could reduce yields.\n\n5. **Formaldehyde concentration**: Pathway 2 requires formaldehyde, which is volatile and reactive. Its prebiotic concentration in hydrothermal environments is debated. Mineral adsorption, continuous production from CO2 reduction via serpentinization-derived H2, and thermophoretic concentration may maintain sufficient steady-state levels.\n\n6. **Alanine as precursor**: Pathway 2 requires L-alanine as a substrate, which must itself be produced by upstream prebiotic reactions. The availability of alanine in sufficient concentrations at hydrothermal vents, where it is simultaneously being consumed by aldol condensation and potentially decomposed at high temperatures, represents a kinetic bottleneck that needs quantitative modeling.\n\n7. **Selectivity and side products**: The aldol condensation of alanine with formaldehyde (Pathway 2) could potentially produce other isomeric products besides threonine. The stereoselectivity and regioselectivity of this reaction under prebiotic conditions, and the relative yields of threonine versus allo-threonine or other isomers, need further characterization.\n\n=== END CURATED LITERATURE REVIEW ===\n\n\n=== END LITERATURE RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\nINSTRUCTIONS FOR LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHESIS:\n1. Prefer routes and conditions that are well supported experimentally in the research above.\n2. Weigh the critiques and contested points the research reports; do not adopt a route uncritically.\n3. You may depart from the described routes when the chemistry justifies it; explain why in \"reasoning\".\n4. Cite the supporting paper (author, year) in each reaction's \"reasoning\" field, or mark the reaction as speculative.\n\n\nPREVIOUS FAILED ATTEMPTS - Learn from this feedback:\n============================================================\n\nAttempt 1:\n- Confidence: 0.66\n- Feedback: Not approved: overall confidence is below the 0.90 network threshold. Formal network validity is high: the 19 molecule nodes and 15 reaction nodes are connected, identifiers are consistent, and the deterministic check establishes that every reaction conserves atoms and charge with suitable coefficients. Explicitly include the supplied coefficients for rxn_002, rxn_010 and rxn_014. The coefficient eight in rxn_002 follows from representing elemental sulfur as S8 and is not an implausible elementary molecularity requirement. Flagged reactions have evidential, kinetic or environmental issues, not conservation violations. The corrected C4 lactaldehyde cyanohydrin, imine-based interpretation of Strecker entry and exclusion of the review's incorrectly mapped alanine-formaldehyde route are chemically justified. Nevertheless, P1-P8 depend on insufficiently established lactaldehyde supply through rxn_002 or rxn_003, followed by processing whose effective conditions and product survival are unspecified. P9-P10 share the substantially weaker glycine-acetaldehyde target-forming step. Anoxia is compatible with the proposal, but Surface and Hydrothermal labels do not establish pH, temperature, pressure, irradiation, catalytic availability or residence time; hydantoin transfer also needs physical justification. Literature alignment is partial: established reaction families support the general strategy, and the network appropriately acknowledges speculation and review errors, but reaction-specific citations and experimental protocols are absent. The supplied review's claims attributed to Patel et al. (2015) and Aubrey et al. (2008) should be checked against primary experimental records rather than accepted as proof of these exact corrected sequences. Unresolved stereochemistry is not penalized because the target is explicitly connectivity-defined. Confidence reflects the connected network's chemical bottlenecks, not its number of pathways, hubs or environments. P2 is the best candidate for validation, not an established end-to-end synthesis.\n- Issues: ['The corrected cyanohydrin and aminonitrile provide the appropriate carbon skeleton for threonine. Cyanohydrin-reservoir entry is plausible through aldehyde release, imine formation and cyanide addition, rather than direct hydroxyl displacement. The principal uncertainties are substrate-specific photoreduction in rxn_002 and effective hydrolysis conditions for rxn_007 and rxn_008. Surface labeling alone does not establish their rates or selectivity.', 'This is the strongest candidate on the information supplied: lactaldehyde Strecker chemistry followed by nitrile and amide hydrolysis is mechanistically coherent and gives the requested connectivity. Its advantage is conditional, not experimental validation. Lactaldehyde supply through rxn_002 remains insufficiently supported, and the network does not specify an operating window that completes hydrolysis while preserving the beta-hydroxy product.', 'Hydantoin formation is a chemically reasonable Bucherer-Bergs-type alternative, and its net stoichiometry is valid. However, aminonitrile trapping by carbon dioxide requires suitable reaction conditions; it is not established merely by listing those reactants. This pathway combines uncertain photoreductive supply with unvalidated hydantoin processing. Transport from the surface formation setting to the hydrothermal setting is not physically specified, and product survival during hydrolysis is unresolved.', 'Direct Strecker entry provides a sound connection between lactaldehyde and the proposed hydantoin precursor. It does not resolve the uncertain lactaldehyde supply, carbon dioxide trapping conditions or terminal hydrothermal conversion. Hydrothermal water alone is not sufficient evidence for useful hydantoin hydrolysis and decarbamoylation, especially when threonine degradation may compete. The surface-to-hydrothermal transfer also remains unexplained.', 'Hydroxyacetone-to-lactaldehyde isomerization is structurally possible through enediol chemistry, but useful lactaldehyde availability is not established. The ketone-aldehyde equilibrium, reaction rate and selective trapping must be quantified. Unconverted hydroxyacetone can undergo competing cyanohydrin or Strecker chemistry, producing a different carbon connectivity. Once lactaldehyde is obtained, the proposed target route is coherent, subject to the unspecified hydrolysis conditions.', 'The lactaldehyde-to-threonine portion is a valid generic Strecker strategy. Its upstream connection remains conditional because rxn_003 has no specified catalyst, pH, equilibrium composition or demonstrated productive flux. Direct Strecker reaction of residual hydroxyacetone would give a constitutional isomer rather than threonine. Final hydrolysis is chemically plausible but cannot be assumed efficient in unspecified surface water.', 'The formal sequence is connected and carbon-consistent, but productive operation depends on three unresolved transformations: selective lactaldehyde supply, hydantoin trapping and hydrothermal release of surviving threonine. Hydantoin formation could provide a useful intermediate reservoir, but that protective function has not been demonstrated for this substrate. Environmental labels do not establish the needed reaction conditions or justify transport between settings.', 'Direct Strecker assembly is plausible if lactaldehyde is available, and the hydantoin route preserves the intended amino-acid skeleton. Nevertheless, rxn_003 has not established sufficient selective precursor supply, while rxn_009 and rxn_010 lack substrate-specific operating evidence. The principal environmental issue is not the use of two environments but the absence of justified transfer and conditions that favor product formation over destruction.', \"The glycine-producing branch uses credible generic chemistry, although hydrolysis conditions remain unspecified. Glycine plus acetaldehyde has the correct carbon connectivity for a threonine aldol addition, unlike the review's alanine-formaldehyde assignment. However, rxn_015 lacks a demonstrated means of activating glycine's alpha-carbon. Imine formation, acetaldehyde self-reaction and retro-aldol chemistry can compete. A balanced equation does not establish useful target production.\", 'Direct formaldehyde Strecker synthesis supplies a credible route to glycine, subject to appropriate hydrolysis conditions. The target-forming step remains the dominant weakness: unactivated glycine is not an efficient carbon nucleophile under generic aqueous surface conditions. The proposed aldol addition is structurally sensible but experimentally and environmentally under-specified. Improving glycine supply alone would not validate threonine production through this branch.']\n\n============================================================\nAddress the above issues in your new network. Do NOT repeat the same mistakes.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"Threonine, connectivity-defined; stereochemistry unresolved\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"Construct the C4 amino-acid skeleton by lactaldehyde Strecker chemistry, followed by stepwise nitrile and amide hydrolysis. The supplied review does not establish ten independent, experimentally demonstrated syntheses. Accordingly, the ten pathways below are explicitly conditional mechanistic and acid/base-processing variants within one synthesis family, not ten independently validated routes. Neutral-imine and iminium entry are alternative mechanistic descriptions requiring kinetic discrimination; cyanohydrin entry represents a different precursor reservoir and mixing history. Correct the review's lactaldehyde cyanohydrin to C4H7NO2: no carbon-skeleton contraction is required. Exclude the proposed alanine-formaldehyde route because ordinary alpha-carbon hydroxymethylation would give a constitutional isomer, not threonine; its attribution to Aubrey et al. (2008) is not adequate evidence for the claimed transformation. Also exclude unsupported lactaldehyde photoreduction/isomerization supply, hydantoin processing, and unactivated glycine-acetaldehyde coupling. Lactaldehyde is an explicit, externally supplied C3 starting material; its geochemical availability remains an unresolved boundary condition, not a demonstrated upstream synthesis. Patel et al. (2015) supplies broader cyanosulfidic context, but the provided review is not reliable reaction-specific verification of this corrected sequence. Numerical conditions below are proposed screening windows, not recovered experimental protocols or established productive operating windows. All reactions are anoxic and use shielded surface porewaters rather than hot vents. Neither enantioselectivity nor threonine/allo-threonine selectivity is established. Each displayed net reaction balances with unit coefficients; the two hydrolysis reactions together consume two waters. Except where explicitly represented, protonation states use neutral analytical reference forms, so this is not a complete aqueous-speciation or proton-budget model.\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H6O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-hydroxypropanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Lactaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H6O2/c1-3(5)2-4/h2-3,5H,1H3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"formula\": \"CHN\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"formula\": \"H3N\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Ammonia\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Ammonia\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Oxidane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Water\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H9NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"1-aminopropane-1,2-diol\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Lactaldehyde-ammonia carbinolamine\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H7NO\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"1-iminopropan-2-ol\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Lactaldehyde imine\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H8NO+\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-hydroxypropan-1-iminium\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Protonated lactaldehyde imine\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"formula\": \"CN-\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Cyanide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Cyanide ion\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CN/c1-2/q-1\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"formula\": \"H4N+\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Azanium\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Ammonium ion\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3/p+1\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H7NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2,3-dihydroxybutanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Lactaldehyde cyanohydrin\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H8N2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-3-hydroxybutanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Threonine aminonitrile precursor\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C4H8N2O/c1-3(7)4(6)2-5/h3-4,7H,6H2,1H3\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H10N2O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-3-hydroxybutanamide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Threoninamide, stereochemistry unspecified\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H9NO3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-3-hydroxybutanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Threonine, stereochemistry unspecified\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C4H9NO3/c1-2(6)3(5)4(7)8/h2-3,6H,5H2,1H3,(H,7,8)\",\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"HCN-ammonia proton-transfer equilibrium\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_002\",\n        \"mol_003\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_008\",\n        \"mol_009\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Reversible equilibrium in anoxic, optically shielded porewater at pH 8-9. Proposed screening concentrations: 10-100 mM total cyanide and 50-500 mM total ammonia. These are test concentrations, not established environmental abundances. Determine free cyanide rather than assuming total cyanide is available; abundant Fe2+ can sequester cyanide. Proton exchange is fast relative to subsequent synthesis.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia accepts a proton from HCN, generating cyanide and ammonium. Both sides remain populated; the reaction arrow does not imply quantitative conversion.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is established acid-base chemistry, not a speculative bond-forming synthesis. Its productive coupling to this lactaldehyde network is unvalidated. HCN-based precursor chemistry is contextualized by Patel et al. (2015), but that citation does not establish the proposed concentrations, free-cyanide activity, or threonine yield.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Ammonia addition to lactaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_001\",\n        \"mol_003\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_005\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic porewater, pH 8-9; proposed lactaldehyde screening range 1-30 mM with excess total ammonia. Screen residence times from 1 hour to 3 days. Water-mediated proton transfers are implicit. Aldehyde hydration, oligomerization, ammonia volatilization, and competing reactions with other carbonyl compounds must be measured.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia adds to the aldehyde carbonyl, followed by proton transfer to form a reversible carbinolamine without changing the C3 skeleton.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Carbinolamine formation is a conventional component of Strecker chemistry (Strecker, 1850). This lactaldehyde-specific implementation is speculative because the supplied Patel et al. (2015) summary does not establish its equilibrium population or rate. The intermediate is included to explain imine formation, not as an assumed isolable reservoir.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Carbinolamine dehydration to neutral imine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_005\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_006\",\n        \"mol_004\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic concentrated aqueous films, approximately pH 8-9. Partial evaporation may concentrate reactants, but complete drying is not assumed beneficial. Proposed observation interval: hours to several days. Imine hydrolysis competes strongly, and productive flux requires subsequent cyanide trapping.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Solvent-assisted proton transfer and water elimination generate the neutral imine. This represents a neutral-imine mechanistic branch, not a demonstrated elementary step with known rate constants.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Imine formation is consistent with classical Strecker chemistry (Strecker, 1850). This substrate-specific branch is speculative: neither the supplied Patel et al. (2015) account nor the review establishes a persistent lactaldehyde imine. Water elimination in aqueous solution cannot be assumed quantitative.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Ammonium protonation of the lactaldehyde imine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_006\",\n        \"mol_009\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_007\",\n        \"mol_003\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic NH3/NH4+-containing porewater at pH 8-9. Proton transfer is reversible. The iminium fraction depends on the unmeasured substrate-specific pKa and must not be equated with the total imine concentration.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonium transfers a proton to imine nitrogen, forming an iminium electrophile and regenerating ammonia.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Iminium formation supplies a chemically defensible electrophile for cyanide attack in the Strecker reaction family (Strecker, 1850). Its quantitative importance here is speculative. Patel et al. (2015) provides framework context rather than a measured lactaldehyde-iminium equilibrium. An unfavorable protonation equilibrium could still permit trapping of a minor reactive population.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanide capture of the lactaldehyde iminium ion\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_007\",\n        \"mol_008\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_011\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic, optically shielded porewater at pH 8-9, with measured free cyanide. Proposed reaction monitoring: minutes to 3 days. Follow aminonitrile formation against imine hydrolysis, cyanide complexation, oligomerization, and retro-Strecker reaction. No UV irradiation or mineral catalyst is required by this bond-forming step.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanide attacks the iminium carbon, producing the C4 alpha-aminonitrile. The cyanide carbon becomes the eventual carboxyl carbon; ammonia supplies the retained amino nitrogen.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the connectivity-correct Strecker construction (Strecker, 1850). Patel et al. (2015) supports the broader relevance of cyanide-derived amino-acid precursors, but the supplied review does not verify this corrected substrate-specific experiment. Target formation is therefore a conditional prediction. Achiral starting material and conditions do not establish L-threonine or exclude allo-threonine precursors.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"General-acid-assisted carbinolamine dehydration directly to iminium\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_005\",\n        \"mol_009\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_007\",\n        \"mol_003\",\n        \"mol_004\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic NH3/NH4+ buffer, approximately pH 8-9, with cyanide available to trap iminium. Proposed observation interval: hours to several days. Ammonium is explicitly represented as the proton donor and is not an additional mineral catalyst. Its effectiveness as a general acid must be tested.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Coupled proton transfer to the departing hydroxyl group and water elimination produce iminium without requiring a separately populated neutral-imine intermediate. Ammonia is released from the proton donor.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"General-acid-assisted carbinolamine dehydration is chemically reasonable within imine-forming chemistry, but this ammonium-assisted lactaldehyde branch is speculative and lacks reaction-specific support in the supplied literature. Classical Strecker chemistry provides context (Strecker, 1850), not kinetic validation. This branch and rxn_003 plus rxn_004 may be experimentally indistinguishable and must not be counted as independently established syntheses.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Formation of the corrected C4 lactaldehyde cyanohydrin\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_001\",\n        \"mol_002\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_010\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic, shielded aqueous film at pH 8-9. Proposed screening concentrations: 1-30 mM lactaldehyde and 10-100 mM total cyanide. Monitor over minutes to 3 days. HCN dissociation supplies the attacking cyanide; no independently added catalyst is assumed. Product formation is reversible.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanide adds to the aldehyde carbonyl, followed by proton transfer. The three-carbon aldehyde plus one cyanide carbon gives 2,3-dihydroxybutanenitrile, C4H7NO2.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Cyanohydrin formation is established reaction chemistry. However, this corrected lactaldehyde-specific application remains unverified by the supplied experimental record. The review attributes the route to Patel et al. (2015), but its C5 product is atom-inconsistent and cannot be adopted. This node provides a plausible precursor reservoir rather than an assumed quantitative or protective intermediate.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanohydrin-reservoir conversion to aminonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_010\",\n        \"mol_003\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_011\",\n        \"mol_004\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic concentrated porewater at pH 8-9 with excess total ammonia; proposed monitoring over 1 hour to 7 days. A cyanohydrin-bearing film must encounter ammoniacal water or receive ammonia before substantial precursor loss. This mixing history is a conditional surface scenario, not a required cross-environment transfer.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The cyanohydrin reversibly releases lactaldehyde and HCN. Ammonia captures the aldehyde through carbinolamine/iminium chemistry, followed by cyanide addition. The displayed equation is the net reservoir-exchange reaction, not direct displacement of hydroxide and not carbon-skeleton rearrangement.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This corrects the review's mechanistically problematic hydroxyl-displacement explanation. Classical Strecker chemistry supports the equilibrium-mediated interpretation (Strecker, 1850), while Patel et al. (2015) supplies broader precursor-network context. Target-specific conversion, equilibrium partitioning, and yield are speculative. This pathway overlaps mechanistically with the explicit iminium branch and is distinguished primarily by precursor storage and mixing history.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Acid-catalyzed aminonitrile hydration to threoninamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_011\",\n        \"mol_004\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_012\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Hydronium ions supplied by non-oxidizing, HCl-rich volcanic water\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm, below local boiling\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium ion\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic, UV-shielded acidic porewater; proposed pH 0-1 at reaction temperature. Screen 6 hours to 14 days, with time-resolved aminonitrile, amide, amino-acid, and degradation measurements. Formation requires an acidic water source and sufficient acid capacity after mixing with ammoniacal precursor water. Amide accumulation is not assumed.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Nitrile protonation facilitates water addition; proton transfers and tautomerization give the amide. One water is consumed in the displayed neutral-reference net reaction.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Acidic aminonitrile hydrolysis is foundational Strecker chemistry (Strecker, 1850), but the proposed temperature, acidity, and residence-time window is a speculative target-specific screening design, not a verified Patel et al. (2015) threonine protocol. Warm acid is specified because useful hydrolysis cannot be assumed in ambient neutral water. Productive operation requires hydrolysis to outcompete fragmentation and loss of the beta-hydroxy skeleton.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Base-catalyzed aminonitrile hydration to threoninamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_011\",\n        \"mol_004\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_012\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Hydroxide ions maintained by alkaline spring water or concentrated carbonate alkalinity\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm, below local boiling\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydroxide ion\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic, shielded alkaline porewater; proposed pH 10.5-11.5 measured at reaction temperature. Screen 1-30 days. Measure alkalinity rather than assuming carbonate guarantees the required pH. Track retro-Strecker loss, side-chain elimination, epimerization, and mineral precipitation. A dilute or weakly alkaline pool may give negligible conversion.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydroxide attacks the nitrile carbon; solvent proton transfers and imidate-to-amide tautomerization yield the amide. This is a distinct catalytic alternative to acid activation.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Base-promoted nitrile hydration is established general chemistry, but this lactaldehyde-derived aminonitrile application and its environmental operating window are speculative. The supplied Patel et al. (2015) account does not demonstrate them. This branch is retained as a chemically meaningful alternative catalyst regime, not evidence of productive prebiotic flux; it should be rejected experimentally if substrate loss outruns hydration.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Acid-catalyzed threoninamide hydrolysis\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_012\",\n        \"mol_004\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_013\",\n        \"mol_003\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Hydronium ions supplied by non-oxidizing, HCl-rich volcanic water\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm, below local boiling\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium ion\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic, shielded aqueous phase; proposed pH 0-1 at reaction temperature. Screen 6 hours to 14 days and measure amino-acid accumulation versus destruction. Acidic hydration and amide hydrolysis may occur in one residence interval without amide isolation. The released nitrogen is predominantly ammonium under these conditions; the equation uses ammonia as its neutral reference form. Cooling or dilution is needed if prolonged warm-acid exposure destroys product.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Water attacks the acid-activated amide carbonyl. Collapse of the tetrahedral intermediate releases ammonia/ammonium and forms the amino-acid carboxyl group while retaining the C4 skeleton.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Amide hydrolysis supplies the second chemically necessary stage of Strecker conversion (Strecker, 1850). The exact threoninamide operating window is speculative and is not verified by the supplied Patel et al. (2015) summary. The review's thermal-stability concerns, citing Vallentyne (1964) and Bada et al. (1978), justify avoiding hot-vent temperatures but do not establish survival here. Formation and destruction rates must both be measured; no stereochemical enrichment is claimed.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Base-catalyzed threoninamide hydrolysis\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_012\",\n        \"mol_004\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_013\",\n        \"mol_003\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Hydroxide ions maintained by alkaline spring water or concentrated carbonate alkalinity\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm, below local boiling\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydroxide ion\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic, shielded alkaline porewater; proposed pH 10.5-11.5 at reaction temperature. Screen 1-30 days. Product is predominantly carboxylate in situ; the molecule node uses the neutral amino-acid reference form. Maintain a measured alkalinity budget and monitor epimerization, retro-aldol fragmentation, and elimination. Productive accumulation requires hydrolysis to outrun these losses.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydroxide addition to the amide carbonyl gives a tetrahedral intermediate, followed by C-N cleavage and formation of amino-acid carboxylate plus ammonia. The displayed equation is the neutral-reference hydrolysis balance.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Base-promoted amide hydrolysis is established general chemistry, but prebiotic release of surviving threonine from this particular amide under the proposed conditions is speculative. No reaction-specific validation is supplied by Patel et al. (2015). This alternative avoids an obligatory strongly acidic work-up, but its advantage cannot be inferred without comparative kinetics and a realistic alkalinity budget.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Neutral-imine entry with continuous acidic hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Conditional reference pathway: lactaldehyde forms a carbinolamine, neutral imine, iminium, and aminonitrile; sequential acidic hydrolysis gives the target connectivity. Precursor formation and acid hydrolysis require separate chemical stages, for example local mixing with HCl-rich volcanic water. No amide isolation is assumed. This is a validation candidate, not an established end-to-end synthesis.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_011\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_006\",\n        \"mol_007\",\n        \"mol_011\",\n        \"mol_012\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Neutral-imine entry with continuous alkaline hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Conditional alternative to P1 using hydroxide-mediated nitrile and amide hydrolysis. Ammoniacal precursor water must encounter sufficiently alkaline, warm porewater. This changes the catalytic regime, not the carbon-construction reaction family. Useful target accumulation is unvalidated.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_010\",\n        \"rxn_012\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_006\",\n        \"mol_011\",\n        \"mol_012\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Neutral-imine entry with acidic hydration and alkaline amide cleavage\",\n      \"description\": \"Speculative staged-processing variant. An acid-generated amide-bearing solution subsequently enters an alkaline surface-porewater zone. It is viable only if appreciable amide survives the first stage and the second zone supplies enough alkalinity after neutralization and dilution. No demonstrated benefit over P1 or P2 is claimed.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_012\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_006\",\n        \"mol_011\",\n        \"mol_012\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Neutral-imine entry with alkaline hydration and acidic amide cleavage\",\n      \"description\": \"Speculative reciprocal pH-staging variant. An alkaline hydration phase must retain enough amide to enter an acidic surface zone before destructive side reactions dominate. Acid capacity, dilution, residence time, and target survival are unresolved; this is not a separately demonstrated synthesis.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_010\",\n        \"rxn_011\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_006\",\n        \"mol_011\",\n        \"mol_012\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct-iminium entry with continuous acidic hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Conditional mechanistic variant in which general-acid-assisted carbinolamine dehydration supplies iminium without an independently populated neutral-imine reservoir. Acidic hydrolysis then supplies the amino acid. The entry mechanism requires experimental discrimination from P1 and does not constitute independent evidence for synthesis.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_011\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_005\",\n        \"mol_007\",\n        \"mol_011\",\n        \"mol_012\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct-iminium entry with continuous alkaline hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Conditional combination of direct iminium-generating dehydration and alkaline nitrile/amide processing. Both the importance of ammonium-assisted dehydration and the hydrolysis-versus-degradation balance remain unmeasured. This is a mechanistic/work-up variant rather than an independent experimentally established route.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_010\",\n        \"rxn_012\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_005\",\n        \"mol_007\",\n        \"mol_011\",\n        \"mol_012\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanohydrin-reservoir entry with continuous acidic hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Conditional reservoir pathway: lactaldehyde first captures HCN, then encounters ammonia and exchanges through aldehyde/iminium equilibria to the aminonitrile. Acidic hydration and amide cleavage follow. Cyanohydrin persistence and productive exchange must be demonstrated; direct hydroxyl substitution is not assumed.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_011\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_010\",\n        \"mol_011\",\n        \"mol_012\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanohydrin-reservoir entry with continuous alkaline hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Conditional cyanohydrin mixing-history variant followed by alkaline processing. The same C4 skeleton is retained throughout. Reservoir protection, aminonitrile yield, and target survival are not established, and free cyanide may be limited by iron complexation.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_010\",\n        \"rxn_012\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_010\",\n        \"mol_011\",\n        \"mol_012\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanohydrin-reservoir entry with acidic hydration and alkaline amide cleavage\",\n      \"description\": \"Speculative staged variant combining cyanohydrin storage/mixing with an acid-to-base processing sequence. Local connected porewater zones, sufficient neutralization capacity, and amide survival would all be necessary. It is a distinct graph path but not a separately supported prebiotic synthesis.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_012\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_010\",\n        \"mol_011\",\n        \"mol_012\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanohydrin-reservoir entry with alkaline hydration and acidic amide cleavage\",\n      \"description\": \"Speculative reciprocal staging variant combining cyanohydrin entry with base-to-acid processing. Its feasibility depends on selective amide production, physically available acidic porewater, and target survival after mixing. Listing this tenth graph path does not increase the evidential confidence of the shared reaction family.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_010\",\n        \"rxn_011\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_010\",\n        \"mol_011\",\n        \"mol_012\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\n    \"mol_005\",\n    \"mol_007\",\n    \"mol_011\",\n    \"mol_012\"\n  ],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_011\",\n        \"rxn_012\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_008\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_008\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_010\",\n        \"rxn_011\",\n        \"rxn_012\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_006\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_005\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_008\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_010\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_010\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_011\",\n        \"rxn_012\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_011\",\n        \"rxn_012\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "Threonine, connectivity-defined; stereochemistry unresolved", "overall_strategy": "Construct the C4 amino-acid skeleton by lactaldehyde Strecker chemistry, followed by stepwise nitrile and amide hydrolysis. The supplied review does not establish ten independent, experimentally demonstrated syntheses. Accordingly, the ten pathways below are explicitly conditional mechanistic and acid/base-processing variants within one synthesis family, not ten independently validated routes. Neutral-imine and iminium entry are alternative mechanistic descriptions requiring kinetic discrimination; cyanohydrin entry represents a different precursor reservoir and mixing history. Correct the review's lactaldehyde cyanohydrin to C4H7NO2: no carbon-skeleton contraction is required. Exclude the proposed alanine-formaldehyde route because ordinary alpha-carbon hydroxymethylation would give a constitutional isomer, not threonine; its attribution to Aubrey et al. (2008) is not adequate evidence for the claimed transformation. Also exclude unsupported lactaldehyde photoreduction/isomerization supply, hydantoin processing, and unactivated glycine-acetaldehyde coupling. Lactaldehyde is an explicit, externally supplied C3 starting material; its geochemical availability remains an unresolved boundary condition, not a demonstrated upstream synthesis. Patel et al. (2015) supplies broader cyanosulfidic context, but the provided review is not reliable reaction-specific verification of this corrected sequence. Numerical conditions below are proposed screening windows, not recovered experimental protocols or established productive operating windows. All reactions are anoxic and use shielded surface porewaters rather than hot vents. Neither enantioselectivity nor threonine/allo-threonine selectivity is established. Each displayed net reaction balances with unit coefficients; the two hydrolysis reactions together consume two waters. Except where explicitly represented, protonation states use neutral analytical reference forms, so this is not a complete aqueous-speciation or proton-budget model.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "C3H6O2", "iupac_name": "2-hydroxypropanal", "common_name": "Lactaldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H6O2/c1-3(5)2-4/h2-3,5H,1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_002", "formula": "CHN", "iupac_name": "Hydrogen cyanide", "common_name": "Hydrogen cyanide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_003", "formula": "H3N", "iupac_name": "Ammonia", "common_name": "Ammonia", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_004", "formula": "H2O", "iupac_name": "Oxidane", "common_name": "Water", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_005", "formula": "C3H9NO2", "iupac_name": "1-aminopropane-1,2-diol", "common_name": "Lactaldehyde-ammonia carbinolamine", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_006", "formula": "C3H7NO", "iupac_name": "1-iminopropan-2-ol", "common_name": "Lactaldehyde imine", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_007", "formula": "C3H8NO+", "iupac_name": "2-hydroxypropan-1-iminium", "common_name": "Protonated lactaldehyde imine", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_008", "formula": "CN-", "iupac_name": "Cyanide", "common_name": "Cyanide ion", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CN/c1-2/q-1", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_009", "formula": "H4N+", "iupac_name": "Azanium", "common_name": "Ammonium ion", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3/p+1", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_010", "formula": "C4H7NO2", "iupac_name": "2,3-dihydroxybutanenitrile", "common_name": "Lactaldehyde cyanohydrin", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_011", "formula": "C4H8N2O", "iupac_name": "2-amino-3-hydroxybutanenitrile", "common_name": "Threonine aminonitrile precursor", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C4H8N2O/c1-3(7)4(6)2-5/h3-4,7H,6H2,1H3", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_012", "formula": "C4H10N2O2", "iupac_name": "2-amino-3-hydroxybutanamide", "common_name": "Threoninamide, stereochemistry unspecified", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_013", "formula": "C4H9NO3", "iupac_name": "2-amino-3-hydroxybutanoic acid", "common_name": "Threonine, stereochemistry unspecified", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C4H9NO3/c1-2(6)3(5)4(7)8/h2-3,6H,5H2,1H3,(H,7,8)", "role": "target", "environment_formed": "Surface"}], "reactions": [{"id": "rxn_001", "name": "HCN-ammonia proton-transfer equilibrium", "inputs": ["mol_002", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_008", "mol_009"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Reversible equilibrium in anoxic, optically shielded porewater at pH 8-9. Proposed screening concentrations: 10-100 mM total cyanide and 50-500 mM total ammonia. These are test concentrations, not established environmental abundances. Determine free cyanide rather than assuming total cyanide is available; abundant Fe2+ can sequester cyanide. Proton exchange is fast relative to subsequent synthesis."}, "mechanism": "Ammonia accepts a proton from HCN, generating cyanide and ammonium. Both sides remain populated; the reaction arrow does not imply quantitative conversion.", "reasoning": "This is established acid-base chemistry, not a speculative bond-forming synthesis. Its productive coupling to this lactaldehyde network is unvalidated. HCN-based precursor chemistry is contextualized by Patel et al. (2015), but that citation does not establish the proposed concentrations, free-cyanide activity, or threonine yield."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Ammonia addition to lactaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_005"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic porewater, pH 8-9; proposed lactaldehyde screening range 1-30 mM with excess total ammonia. Screen residence times from 1 hour to 3 days. Water-mediated proton transfers are implicit. Aldehyde hydration, oligomerization, ammonia volatilization, and competing reactions with other carbonyl compounds must be measured."}, "mechanism": "Ammonia adds to the aldehyde carbonyl, followed by proton transfer to form a reversible carbinolamine without changing the C3 skeleton.", "reasoning": "Carbinolamine formation is a conventional component of Strecker chemistry (Strecker, 1850). This lactaldehyde-specific implementation is speculative because the supplied Patel et al. (2015) summary does not establish its equilibrium population or rate. The intermediate is included to explain imine formation, not as an assumed isolable reservoir."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Carbinolamine dehydration to neutral imine", "inputs": ["mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_006", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic concentrated aqueous films, approximately pH 8-9. Partial evaporation may concentrate reactants, but complete drying is not assumed beneficial. Proposed observation interval: hours to several days. Imine hydrolysis competes strongly, and productive flux requires subsequent cyanide trapping."}, "mechanism": "Solvent-assisted proton transfer and water elimination generate the neutral imine. This represents a neutral-imine mechanistic branch, not a demonstrated elementary step with known rate constants.", "reasoning": "Imine formation is consistent with classical Strecker chemistry (Strecker, 1850). This substrate-specific branch is speculative: neither the supplied Patel et al. (2015) account nor the review establishes a persistent lactaldehyde imine. Water elimination in aqueous solution cannot be assumed quantitative."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Ammonium protonation of the lactaldehyde imine", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_009"], "outputs": ["mol_007", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic NH3/NH4+-containing porewater at pH 8-9. Proton transfer is reversible. The iminium fraction depends on the unmeasured substrate-specific pKa and must not be equated with the total imine concentration."}, "mechanism": "Ammonium transfers a proton to imine nitrogen, forming an iminium electrophile and regenerating ammonia.", "reasoning": "Iminium formation supplies a chemically defensible electrophile for cyanide attack in the Strecker reaction family (Strecker, 1850). Its quantitative importance here is speculative. Patel et al. (2015) provides framework context rather than a measured lactaldehyde-iminium equilibrium. An unfavorable protonation equilibrium could still permit trapping of a minor reactive population."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Cyanide capture of the lactaldehyde iminium ion", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_008"], "outputs": ["mol_011"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic, optically shielded porewater at pH 8-9, with measured free cyanide. Proposed reaction monitoring: minutes to 3 days. Follow aminonitrile formation against imine hydrolysis, cyanide complexation, oligomerization, and retro-Strecker reaction. No UV irradiation or mineral catalyst is required by this bond-forming step."}, "mechanism": "Cyanide attacks the iminium carbon, producing the C4 alpha-aminonitrile. The cyanide carbon becomes the eventual carboxyl carbon; ammonia supplies the retained amino nitrogen.", "reasoning": "This is the connectivity-correct Strecker construction (Strecker, 1850). Patel et al. (2015) supports the broader relevance of cyanide-derived amino-acid precursors, but the supplied review does not verify this corrected substrate-specific experiment. Target formation is therefore a conditional prediction. Achiral starting material and conditions do not establish L-threonine or exclude allo-threonine precursors."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "General-acid-assisted carbinolamine dehydration directly to iminium", "inputs": ["mol_005", "mol_009"], "outputs": ["mol_007", "mol_003", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic NH3/NH4+ buffer, approximately pH 8-9, with cyanide available to trap iminium. Proposed observation interval: hours to several days. Ammonium is explicitly represented as the proton donor and is not an additional mineral catalyst. Its effectiveness as a general acid must be tested."}, "mechanism": "Coupled proton transfer to the departing hydroxyl group and water elimination produce iminium without requiring a separately populated neutral-imine intermediate. Ammonia is released from the proton donor.", "reasoning": "General-acid-assisted carbinolamine dehydration is chemically reasonable within imine-forming chemistry, but this ammonium-assisted lactaldehyde branch is speculative and lacks reaction-specific support in the supplied literature. Classical Strecker chemistry provides context (Strecker, 1850), not kinetic validation. This branch and rxn_003 plus rxn_004 may be experimentally indistinguishable and must not be counted as independently established syntheses."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Formation of the corrected C4 lactaldehyde cyanohydrin", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_010"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic, shielded aqueous film at pH 8-9. Proposed screening concentrations: 1-30 mM lactaldehyde and 10-100 mM total cyanide. Monitor over minutes to 3 days. HCN dissociation supplies the attacking cyanide; no independently added catalyst is assumed. Product formation is reversible."}, "mechanism": "Cyanide adds to the aldehyde carbonyl, followed by proton transfer. The three-carbon aldehyde plus one cyanide carbon gives 2,3-dihydroxybutanenitrile, C4H7NO2.", "reasoning": "Cyanohydrin formation is established reaction chemistry. However, this corrected lactaldehyde-specific application remains unverified by the supplied experimental record. The review attributes the route to Patel et al. (2015), but its C5 product is atom-inconsistent and cannot be adopted. This node provides a plausible precursor reservoir rather than an assumed quantitative or protective intermediate."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Cyanohydrin-reservoir conversion to aminonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_010", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_011", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic concentrated porewater at pH 8-9 with excess total ammonia; proposed monitoring over 1 hour to 7 days. A cyanohydrin-bearing film must encounter ammoniacal water or receive ammonia before substantial precursor loss. This mixing history is a conditional surface scenario, not a required cross-environment transfer."}, "mechanism": "The cyanohydrin reversibly releases lactaldehyde and HCN. Ammonia captures the aldehyde through carbinolamine/iminium chemistry, followed by cyanide addition. The displayed equation is the net reservoir-exchange reaction, not direct displacement of hydroxide and not carbon-skeleton rearrangement.", "reasoning": "This corrects the review's mechanistically problematic hydroxyl-displacement explanation. Classical Strecker chemistry supports the equilibrium-mediated interpretation (Strecker, 1850), while Patel et al. (2015) supplies broader precursor-network context. Target-specific conversion, equilibrium partitioning, and yield are speculative. This pathway overlaps mechanistically with the explicit iminium branch and is distinguished primarily by precursor storage and mixing history."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Acid-catalyzed aminonitrile hydration to threoninamide", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_012"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydronium ions supplied by non-oxidizing, HCl-rich volcanic water"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-370 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm, below local boiling", "catalyst": "Hydronium ion", "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic, UV-shielded acidic porewater; proposed pH 0-1 at reaction temperature. Screen 6 hours to 14 days, with time-resolved aminonitrile, amide, amino-acid, and degradation measurements. Formation requires an acidic water source and sufficient acid capacity after mixing with ammoniacal precursor water. Amide accumulation is not assumed."}, "mechanism": "Nitrile protonation facilitates water addition; proton transfers and tautomerization give the amide. One water is consumed in the displayed neutral-reference net reaction.", "reasoning": "Acidic aminonitrile hydrolysis is foundational Strecker chemistry (Strecker, 1850), but the proposed temperature, acidity, and residence-time window is a speculative target-specific screening design, not a verified Patel et al. (2015) threonine protocol. Warm acid is specified because useful hydrolysis cannot be assumed in ambient neutral water. Productive operation requires hydrolysis to outcompete fragmentation and loss of the beta-hydroxy skeleton."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Base-catalyzed aminonitrile hydration to threoninamide", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_012"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydroxide ions maintained by alkaline spring water or concentrated carbonate alkalinity"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-370 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm, below local boiling", "catalyst": "Hydroxide ion", "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic, shielded alkaline porewater; proposed pH 10.5-11.5 measured at reaction temperature. Screen 1-30 days. Measure alkalinity rather than assuming carbonate guarantees the required pH. Track retro-Strecker loss, side-chain elimination, epimerization, and mineral precipitation. A dilute or weakly alkaline pool may give negligible conversion."}, "mechanism": "Hydroxide attacks the nitrile carbon; solvent proton transfers and imidate-to-amide tautomerization yield the amide. This is a distinct catalytic alternative to acid activation.", "reasoning": "Base-promoted nitrile hydration is established general chemistry, but this lactaldehyde-derived aminonitrile application and its environmental operating window are speculative. The supplied Patel et al. (2015) account does not demonstrate them. This branch is retained as a chemically meaningful alternative catalyst regime, not evidence of productive prebiotic flux; it should be rejected experimentally if substrate loss outruns hydration."}, {"id": "rxn_011", "name": "Acid-catalyzed threoninamide hydrolysis", "inputs": ["mol_012", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_013", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydronium ions supplied by non-oxidizing, HCl-rich volcanic water"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-370 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm, below local boiling", "catalyst": "Hydronium ion", "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic, shielded aqueous phase; proposed pH 0-1 at reaction temperature. Screen 6 hours to 14 days and measure amino-acid accumulation versus destruction. Acidic hydration and amide hydrolysis may occur in one residence interval without amide isolation. The released nitrogen is predominantly ammonium under these conditions; the equation uses ammonia as its neutral reference form. Cooling or dilution is needed if prolonged warm-acid exposure destroys product."}, "mechanism": "Water attacks the acid-activated amide carbonyl. Collapse of the tetrahedral intermediate releases ammonia/ammonium and forms the amino-acid carboxyl group while retaining the C4 skeleton.", "reasoning": "Amide hydrolysis supplies the second chemically necessary stage of Strecker conversion (Strecker, 1850). The exact threoninamide operating window is speculative and is not verified by the supplied Patel et al. (2015) summary. The review's thermal-stability concerns, citing Vallentyne (1964) and Bada et al. (1978), justify avoiding hot-vent temperatures but do not establish survival here. Formation and destruction rates must both be measured; no stereochemical enrichment is claimed."}, {"id": "rxn_012", "name": "Base-catalyzed threoninamide hydrolysis", "inputs": ["mol_012", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_013", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydroxide ions maintained by alkaline spring water or concentrated carbonate alkalinity"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-370 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm, below local boiling", "catalyst": "Hydroxide ion", "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic, shielded alkaline porewater; proposed pH 10.5-11.5 at reaction temperature. Screen 1-30 days. Product is predominantly carboxylate in situ; the molecule node uses the neutral amino-acid reference form. Maintain a measured alkalinity budget and monitor epimerization, retro-aldol fragmentation, and elimination. Productive accumulation requires hydrolysis to outrun these losses."}, "mechanism": "Hydroxide addition to the amide carbonyl gives a tetrahedral intermediate, followed by C-N cleavage and formation of amino-acid carboxylate plus ammonia. The displayed equation is the neutral-reference hydrolysis balance.", "reasoning": "Base-promoted amide hydrolysis is established general chemistry, but prebiotic release of surviving threonine from this particular amide under the proposed conditions is speculative. No reaction-specific validation is supplied by Patel et al. (2015). This alternative avoids an obligatory strongly acidic work-up, but its advantage cannot be inferred without comparative kinetics and a realistic alkalinity budget."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "Neutral-imine entry with continuous acidic hydrolysis", "description": "Conditional reference pathway: lactaldehyde forms a carbinolamine, neutral imine, iminium, and aminonitrile; sequential acidic hydrolysis gives the target connectivity. Precursor formation and acid hydrolysis require separate chemic