m cited studies?\n- Are literature citations present in reaction reasoning?\n- Does the network acknowledge critiques and known challenges identified in the research?\n- Departures from the research are acceptable when explicitly and soundly justified.\n\n## Scoring Guidelines\n- 0.9-1.0: Excellent network, valid and chemically sound\n- 0.8-0.9: Good network with minor issues\n- 0.7-0.8: Acceptable but with notable concerns\n- 0.5-0.7: Significant problems with validity or chemistry\n- 0.0-0.5: Major issues, network is not viable\n\nA network is APPROVED if overall_confidence >= 0.9\n\nOutput your evaluation as JSON matching the NetworkEvaluation schema.\n", "user_message": "Evaluate this synthesis network:\n\nTARGET MOLECULE: Threonine, connectivity-defined; stereochemistry unresolved\nOVERALL STRATEGY: Construct the C4 amino-acid skeleton by lactaldehyde Strecker chemistry, followed by stepwise nitrile and amide hydrolysis. The supplied review does not establish ten independent, experimentally demonstrated syntheses. Accordingly, the ten pathways below are explicitly conditional mechanistic and acid/base-processing variants within one synthesis family, not ten independently validated routes. Neutral-imine and iminium entry are alternative mechanistic descriptions requiring kinetic discrimination; cyanohydrin entry represents a different precursor reservoir and mixing history. Correct the review's lactaldehyde cyanohydrin to C4H7NO2: no carbon-skeleton contraction is required. Exclude the proposed alanine-formaldehyde route because ordinary alpha-carbon hydroxymethylation would give a constitutional isomer, not threonine; its attribution to Aubrey et al. (2008) is not adequate evidence for the claimed transformation. Also exclude unsupported lactaldehyde photoreduction/isomerization supply, hydantoin processing, and unactivated glycine-acetaldehyde coupling. Lactaldehyde is an explicit, externally supplied C3 starting material; its geochemical availability remains 