hydantoin\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_016\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H9N2O4-\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-(carbamoylamino)-3-hydroxybutanoate\",\n      \"common_name\": \"N-carbamoylthreoninate\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_017\",\n      \"formula\": \"CO3^2-\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Carbonate\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Carbonate\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct lactaldehyde Strecker assembly\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-315 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screen: pH 8.0-9.2; total lactaldehyde 1-20 mM, uncomplexed HCN/CN- pool 10-100 mM, total NH3/NH4+ 50-500 mM; 6 hours to 7 days. Use anoxic, water-filled pores below UV-attenuating sediment or rock cover. Measure free carbonyl and free cyanide rather than assuming their analytical totals are reactive. Quantify ammonia/HCN volatilization and Fe2+-cyanide sequestration.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Net: mol_001 + mol_002 + mol_003 -> mol_006 + mol_004. Reversible ammonia addition and dehydration establish a transient imine/iminium population, which cyanide captures. The C3 aldehyde supplies the side chain and alpha carbon; cyanide supplies the future carboxyl carbon.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The Strecker reaction family is established (Strecker, 1850), and Patel et al. (2015) provides relevant cyanosulfidic context. However, the supplied review does not verify a threonine-specific protocol with these concentrations, rates or yields. This corrected substrate-specific application and its environmental operating window remain provisional. Lactaldehyde hydration, oligomerization, reversible imine formation and competing cyanohydrin formation must be measured. No enantioselectivity is inferred.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Formation of the correctly sized lactaldehyde cyanohydrin\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Hydroxide from mildly alkaline porewater, acting as a general-base catalyst\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-310 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydroxide, general-base catalysis\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screen: pH 8.0-9.0; lactaldehyde 1-20 mM and uncomplexed HCN/CN- 10-100 mM; 1 hour to 3 days. Initially use ammonia-poor water to distinguish cyanohydrin storage from simultaneous Strecker chemistry. Maintain anoxia and local UV shielding. Determine the equilibrium cyanohydrin fraction and losses rather than assuming quantitative trapping.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Net: mol_001 + mol_002 -> mol_005. Cyanide adds to the aldehyde carbonyl, followed by proton transfer. The product is CH3-CH(OH)-CH(OH)-CN and contains four carbons.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Carbonyl cyanohydrin formation supports this connectivity. The supplied attribution to Patel et al. (2015) contains a carbon-count error: a C3 aldehyde plus HCN cannot directly produce the listed C5 cyanohydrin. The corrected C4 reaction is chemically justified, but its substrate-specific equilibrium and geochemical yield are not established by the supplied evidence. Its proposed reservoir function is therefore speculative.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanohydrin-reservoir conversion through Strecker equilibration\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-315 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screen: cyanohydrin 1-20 mM, total NH3/NH4+ 50-500 mM, pH 8.0-9.2; 1-14 days. Introduce ammonia-bearing water into the same sheltered surface porewater, accounting for dilution. Track lactaldehyde, cyanohydrin and aminonitrile separately. Do not proceed on the assumption that disappearance of cyanohydrin equals aminonitrile production.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Net: mol_005 + mol_003 -> mol_006 + mol_004. This is a composite equilibrium route: cyanohydrin dissociation releases lactaldehyde and HCN, lactaldehyde forms an imine with ammonia, and cyanide captures the imine/iminium species. It is not an elementary substitution of an unactivated hydroxyl group.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This interpretation is consistent with Strecker chemistry (Strecker, 1850) and avoids the unsupported displacement and carbon-skeleton rearrangement described in the supplied Patel et al. (2015) summary. Productive reservoir-to-aminonitrile flux remains speculative without substrate-specific kinetic measurements. This entry history shares the microscopic Strecker chemistry of rxn_001 and is not independent evidence for a second reaction family.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Acid-assisted aminonitrile hydration to threoninamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Hydronium supplied by acidic volcanic condensate\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screen: pH 0.5-1.5, aminonitrile 0.1-5 mM after mixing, and 30-300 mM free strong-acid capacity after neutralizing carried-over ammonia and alkalinity; sample over 6 hours to 30 days. Volcanic HCl-bearing condensate is an anoxia-compatible acid source, not oxygen-dependent sulfide weathering. Use shaded, water-filled hot-spring margins. An amide-rich fraction must be demonstrated before assigning a separate downstream processing stage.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Net: mol_006 + mol_004 -> mol_007. Proton-assisted water addition to the nitrile, followed by proton transfers and tautomerization, forms the carboxamide. Aminonitrile and amide formulas are neutral analytical reference forms; their reversible amino-group protonation is not expanded into additional nodes.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Stepwise nitrile-to-amide-to-acid conversion belongs to the established Strecker hydrolysis family (Strecker, 1850). The supplied Patel et al. (2015) summary does not provide a verified threonine-aminonitrile hydrolysis protocol. These numerical conditions are consequently speculative screening conditions, not established operating conditions. Useful hydration must outrun retro-Strecker chemistry and decomposition; selective amide accumulation is not assumed.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Alkaline aminonitrile hydration to threoninamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Hydroxide supplied by hyperalkaline surface spring water\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydroxide, for the hydration step\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screen: free OH- 3-30 mM, corresponding approximately to pH 11.5-12.5 at 298 K; aminonitrile 0.1-5 mM after mixing; 6 hours to 30 days. Hot-fluid pH must be measured or calculated using temperature-dependent water ionization, not equated to the 298 K value. Determine available alkalinity after carbonate precipitation and acid carryover. Serpentinization-fed surface springs are a possible source, not a guaranteed reservoir.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Net: mol_006 + mol_004 -> mol_007. Hydroxide addition to the nitrile and subsequent proton transfers give the amide. Hydroxide may be regenerated during this hydration step; its net consumption during subsequent amide cleavage is explicitly represented in rxn_007.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is a chemically plausible but substrate-specific speculative alternative. The supplied Patel et al. (2015) account does not establish alkaline hydration rates, an amide yield, or product survival for this aminonitrile. The stated temperature and hydroxide concentrations are a test envelope only. Competing degradation and stereochemical scrambling must be quantified; a stronger base requirement would weaken the geochemical case.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Acidic threoninamide hydrolysis with explicit proton uptake\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_009\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Hydronium as acid promoter; net proton uptake is included in the reaction\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium; acid promotion does not imply zero net acid consumption\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screen: pH 0.5-1.5, threoninamide 0.01-5 mM, 30-300 mM residual free strong-acid capacity; 1-30 days with time-resolved analysis. The displayed net reaction requires two acid equivalents per neutral amide, in addition to neutralizing other carried-over bases. For a preceding alkaline stage, cool and mix the entire intermediate-bearing solution with acidic condensate; include the resulting dilution. Move product-containing water to a 300-315 K zone within one hour after the selected residence interval.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Net: mol_007 + 2 mol_009 -> mol_012 + mol_011 + mol_004. Water attacks the proton-activated amide; C-N cleavage and proton transfers produce protonated threonine and ammonium. The two hydronium equivalents account for the displayed final ionic products.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Amide hydrolysis is chemically well founded, but the supplied Patel et al. (2015) summary does not establish this operating window. Analytical amino-acid hydrolysis literature documents the need to account for hydrolysis-related losses (Fountoulakis and Lahm, 1998); it does not validate this prebiotic threoninamide conversion. This environmental application remains speculative. Threonine-spike degradation controls and independent product identification are required to establish net accumulation rather than merely amide disappearance.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Base-promoted threoninamide hydrolysis\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_010\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screen: free OH- 3-30 mM, approximately pH 11.5-12.5 at 298 K, and threoninamide 0.01-5 mM; 1-30 days. Supply at least one net hydroxide equivalent per amide plus the separately measured demand from residual acid, ammonium and carbonate chemistry. For an acidic precursor stream, cool before mixing with alkaline spring water and calculate concentration after dilution. Terminate the hot exposure by transfer into adjacent cooler water; no selective mineral retention is assumed.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Net: mol_007 + mol_010 -> mol_013 + mol_003. Hydroxide attacks the amide carbonyl, and collapse of the tetrahedral intermediate produces carboxylate and ammonia. This is net base-consuming hydrolysis, not a catalytic hydroxide cycle.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The net chemistry is standard amide hydrolysis, but no substrate-specific alkaline protocol is established in the supplied research; this environmental application is speculative. Patel et al. (2015) supplies broader amino-acid-precursor context rather than evidence for the stated rate or yield. Explicit hydroxide consumption corrects the alkalinity omission in earlier variants. The pathway fails if hydrolysis is too slow or degradation exceeds surviving threoninate production.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Neutralization of acidic threonine product\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_010\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-315 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Mix cooled acidic product solution with anoxia-compatible alkaline surface water to a final pH of 5-7. The displayed substrate neutralization consumes one hydroxide equivalent per threoninium ion. Additional base must neutralize free acidity and other titratable species; determine that demand by a full titration balance. Mixing and proton equilibration are rapid relative to hydrolysis, but the actual residence and dilution depend on the physical site.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Net: mol_012 + mol_010 -> mol_008 + mol_004. Deprotonation of the carboxylic acid gives the electrically neutral, predominantly zwitterionic amino-acid pool. This changes protonation state, not carbon connectivity or stereochemistry.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Proton transfer is established acid-base chemistry, not an additional amino-acid synthesis mechanism. Its particular geochemical implementation is speculative and is not documented by the supplied Patel et al. (2015) summary. This node makes acidic product speciation and the minimum neutralization demand explicit; it does not imply crystallization, purification or chiral resolution.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Neutralization of alkaline threonine product\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_009\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-315 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Mix cooled alkaline product water with acidic volcanic condensate to a final pH of 5-7. The displayed target transformation consumes one hydronium equivalent per threoninate. The total acid demand also includes remaining hydroxide, ammonia protonation and carbonate neutralization; allow approximately one additional equivalent per retained NH3 and up to two per carbonate when it is converted to dissolved CO2. Report final concentrations after mixing rather than assuming quantitative concentration or retention.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Net: mol_013 + mol_009 -> mol_008 + mol_004. Protonation of the amino group gives the predominantly zwitterionic target pool. Other solution-buffer neutralizations contribute to the bulk acid budget but are not additional target-forming transformations.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The proton-transfer chemistry is established; the environmental mixing history is speculative and is not a reported threonine protocol in Patel et al. (2015). Explicitly accounting for the target protonation step avoids treating neutral threonine as the immediate dominant species in strongly alkaline hydrolysis. It does not solve the preceding kinetic bottleneck.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Candidate Bucherer-Bergs hydantoin trapping\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_014\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-350 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screen: aminonitrile 1-10 mM, total dissolved inorganic carbon 20-100 mM, total NH3/NH4+ 20-100 mM and pH 8.0-9.2; 1-14 days. Distinguish total inorganic carbon from free dissolved CO2. Use anoxic, UV-shielded carbonate/ammonium-bearing porewater. Identify the substituted hydantoin directly; precipitation, protection and quantitative trapping are not assumed.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Net: mol_006 + mol_014 -> mol_015. Aminonitrile carbamylation, intramolecular cyclization and rearrangement can assemble the hydantoin ring. The net transformation incorporates CO2 without net water consumption; it is not a single elementary collision.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Bucherer-Bergs chemistry supplies a genuine alternative reaction family for carbonyl-derived aminonitrile trapping (Bucherer and Lieb, 1934). Neither that general precedent nor the supplied Patel et al. (2015) summary establishes this beta-hydroxy substrate under the stated conditions. This branch is speculative and is included specifically to test whether a persistent precursor reservoir can mitigate aminonitrile loss. Any protective benefit and the eventual recoverability of threonine must be demonstrated.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Candidate alkaline hydantoin ring opening\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_010\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_016\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"360-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed lower-temperature screen: hydantoin 0.01-1 mM after fluid mixing, free OH- 3-30 mM, approximately pH 11.5-12.5 at 298 K; 1-90 days. Introduce alkaline spring water into the same surface setting rather than invoking deep-vent transport. Quantify the alkalinity consumed by the original ammonium/carbonate medium and dilution during replacement. Require direct identification of N-carbamoylthreoninate; hotter or more concentrated preparative conditions are not silently substituted.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Net: mol_015 + mol_010 -> mol_016. Hydroxide-promoted acyl cleavage opens the hydantoin ring to the N-carbamoyl amino-acid carboxylate. One hydroxide equivalent is consumed in the displayed neutral-hydantoin reference equation.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Hydantoin-to-carbamoyl-amino-acid chemistry is consistent with the broader Bucherer-Bergs framework (Bucherer and Lieb, 1934), but the supplied research does not establish this substrate-specific surface reaction. The stated screen is speculative and may fail because useful ring opening can require harsher conditions. No useful residence time, protective effect or product survival is claimed in advance.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Candidate alkaline decarbamoylation to threoninate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_010\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_017\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"360-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed feasibility screen only: N-carbamoylthreoninate 0.01-1 mM, free OH- 3-30 mM, approximately pH 11.5-12.5 at 298 K; 1-90 days. Budget two additional hydroxide equivalents per substrate when the released inorganic carbon is represented as carbonate. Monitor substrate, threoninate, ammonia, inorganic carbon and degradation products. A threonine-spike survival control is essential. Reject this branch if net target accumulation cannot be shown within the permitted window.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Net: mol_016 + 2 mol_010 -> mol_013 + mol_003 + mol_017. Hydrolysis of the carbamoyl group releases the amino acid and ammonia; alkaline capture of the removed carbonyl carbon as carbonate gives the displayed net hydroxide demand.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative and the weakest target-releasing step in this network: the supplied research contains no verified nonenzymatic protocol for this substrate at these temperatures and alkalinities. Generic hydantoin chemistry is not sufficient evidence that the carbamoyl group can be removed before beta-hydroxy amino-acid degradation. The relatively resistant carbamoyl linkage may make this branch inoperative in surface waters. P9 and P10 therefore remain conditional alternatives, not validated prebiotic routes.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct Strecker entry with continuously acidic hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Conditional reference pathway: lactaldehyde undergoes Strecker assembly, followed by nitrile hydration and amide cleavage in acidic water, then cooling and neutralization. No isolation of the amide is required. Productive aminonitrile formation and net threonine survival remain unverified.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct Strecker entry with continuously alkaline hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Conditional alkaline alternative. Nitrile hydration is followed by base-consuming amide cleavage and final protonation. Available alkalinity must be demonstrated after buffering and precipitation losses; no rate advantage over P1 is asserted.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct Strecker entry with acidic hydration and alkaline cleavage\",\n      \"description\": \"Conditional split-stage history testing whether the preferred operating windows for nitrile hydration and amide cleavage differ. Requires a measured amide inventory, cooling, neutralization of the acidic liquor and mixing with sufficient alkaline water. Dilution is carried forward; improved yield is not assumed.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct Strecker entry with alkaline hydration and acidic cleavage\",\n      \"description\": \"Conditional reverse split-stage history. An experimentally demonstrated amide-containing alkaline solution is cooled and mixed with acidic condensate before amide cleavage. Acid supply must exceed the complete carried-over alkalinity and ammonia demand.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanohydrin-reservoir entry with acidic hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Cyanohydrin formation precedes ammonia delivery, followed by reversible release and Strecker trapping. The aminonitrile then follows acidic hydration and cleavage. This is distinct as a storage and feedstock-delivery history, not as an independent microscopic Strecker mechanism.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanohydrin-reservoir entry with alkaline hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Conditional reservoir-first entry followed by alkaline nitrile and amide processing. Residual cyanohydrin must be distinguished from aminonitrile because its hydrolysis produces non-amino products. The base and product-survival requirements of P2 also apply.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanohydrin-reservoir entry with acidic hydration and alkaline cleavage\",\n      \"description\": \"Conditional combination of reservoir release and acid-to-base processing. Requires measured conversion to aminonitrile and then a persistent amide pool before the pH change. Neutralization, dilution and substrate recovery must be quantified; pathway enumeration is not additional experimental evidence.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanohydrin-reservoir entry with alkaline hydration and acidic cleavage\",\n      \"description\": \"Conditional reservoir-first route with a base-to-acid processing transition. The same surface solution carries its organic intermediates between stages; no selective mineral adsorption or unexplained cross-environment transport is assumed. Its performance relative to continuous-pH processing is unknown.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct Strecker entry with hydantoin trapping and release\",\n      \"description\": \"Lower-evidence candidate: aminonitrile is trapped as a hydantoin, opened to N-carbamoylthreoninate and subjected to decarbamoylation before neutralization. This tests a chemically distinct reservoir hypothesis. It cannot be considered operational unless rxn_011 and especially rxn_012 release surviving target under the stated constraints.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_015\", \"mol_016\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanohydrin-first entry with hydantoin trapping and release\",\n      \"description\": \"Lower-evidence candidate combining two sequential storage hypotheses: a cyanohydrin reservoir followed by hydantoin trapping. It has the additional dependency of productive cyanohydrin-to-aminonitrile transfer and shares the unresolved carbamoyl-release bottleneck of P9. No protective or kinetic superiority is claimed.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_015\", \"mol_016\", \"mol_013\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_013\"],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_010\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_007\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_009\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "Threonine", "overall_strategy": "An evidence-qualified network containing 17 molecule nodes, 12 reaction nodes and 10 distinct reaction-sequence pathways. The ten pathways are candidate processing histories, not ten experimentally demonstrated syntheses: two Strecker entry histories combine with four acid/base hydrolysis histories, while two additional pathways examine hydantoin trapping and release. The supplied review does not establish ten independent routes or provide reaction-specific operating protocols. Its lactaldehyde cyanohydrin must be C4H7NO2, not C5H9NO2; cyanohydrin conversion is modeled through reversible aldehyde release and imine capture, not direct hydroxyl displacement. Alanine plus formaldehyde is excluded because ordinary alpha-carbon addition gives the wrong connectivity. Lactaldehyde is an explicit external C3 feedstock; its geochemical supply remains unresolved rather than being filled by an unsupported upstream reaction. All numerical conditions below are proposed experimental screening windows, not reported successful protocols or predicted yields. All stages use anoxic surface waters or shallow, UV-shielded porewaters. Acid and alkaline fluids are supplied separately; mixing, neutralization and dilution cannot be ignored. The target is connectivity-defined: neither L-selectivity nor separation of threonine from allothreonine is established. Molecular equations describe net transformations, not exhaustive aqueous speciation. Spectator counterions are omitted, but net charge and atom conservation are maintained. Organic branching and convergence nodes are identified separately from ubiquitous water and acid/base reagents.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "C3H6O2", "iupac_name": "2-hydroxypropanal", "common_name": "Lactaldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H6O2/c1-3(5)2-4/h2-3,5H,1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_002", "formula": "CHN", "iupac_name": "Hydrogen cyanide", "common_name": "Hydrogen cyanide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_003", "formula": "NH3", "iupac_name": "Azane", "common_name": "Ammonia", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_004", "formula": "H2O", "iupac_name": "Water", "common_name": "Water", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_005", "formula": "C4H7NO2", "iupac_name": "2,3-dihydroxybutanenitrile", "common_name": "Lactaldehyde cyanohydrin", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_006", "formula": "C4H8N2O", "iupac_name": "2-amino-3-hydroxybutanenitrile", "common_name": "Threonine aminonitrile", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C4H8N2O/c1-3(7)4(6)2-5/h3-4,7H,6H2,1H3", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_007", "formula": "C4H10N2O2", "iupac_name": "2-amino-3-hydroxybutanamide", "common_name": "Threoninamide", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_008", "formula": "C4H9NO3", "iupac_name": "2-amino-3-hydroxybutanoic acid", "common_name": "Threonine, stereochemistry unspecified", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C4H9NO3/c1-2(6)3(5)4(7)8/h2-3,6H,5H2,1H3,(H,7,8)", "role": "target", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_009", "formula": "H3O+", "iupac_name": "Oxidanium", "common_name": "Hydronium", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2/p+1", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_010", "formula": "OH-", "iupac_name": "Hydroxide", "common_name": "Hydroxide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2/p-1", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_011", "formula": "NH4+", "iupac_name": "Azanium", "common_name": "Ammonium", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3/p+1", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_012", "formula": "C4H10NO3+", "iupac_name": "1-carboxy-2-hydroxypropan-1-aminium", "common_name": "Threoninium", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_013", "formula": "C4H8NO3-", "iupac_name": "2-amino-3-hydroxybutanoate", "common_name": "Threoninate", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_014", "formula": "CO2", "iupac_name": "Carbon dioxide", "common_name": "Carbon dioxide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CO2/c1-2-3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_015", "formula": "C5H8N2O3", "iupac_name": "5-(1-hydroxyethyl)imidazolidine-2,4-dione", "common_name": "Threonine hydantoin", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_016", "formula": "C5H9N2O4-", "iupac_name": "2-(carbamoylamino)-3-hydroxybutanoate", "common_name": "N-carbamoylthreoninate", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_017", "formula": "CO3^2-", "iupac_name": "Carbonate", "common_name": "Carbonate", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}], "reactions": [{"id": "rxn_001", "name": "Direct lactaldehyde Strecker assembly", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_002", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_006", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-315 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: pH 8.0-9.2; total lactaldehyde 1-20 mM, uncomplexed HCN/CN- pool 10-100 mM, total NH3/NH4+ 50-500 mM; 6 hours to 7 days. Use anoxic, water-filled pores below UV-attenuating sediment or rock cover. Measure free carbonyl and free cyanide rather than assuming their analytical totals are reactive. Quantify ammonia/HCN volatilization and Fe2+-cyanide sequestration."}, "mechanism": "Net: mol_001 + mol_002 + mol_003 -> mol_006 + mol_004. Reversible ammonia addition and dehydration establish a transient imine/iminium population, which cyanide captures. The C3 aldehyde supplies the side chain and alpha carbon; cyanide supplies the future carboxyl carbon.", "reasoning": "The Strecker reaction family is established (Strecker, 1850), and Patel et al. (2015) provides relevant cyanosulfidic context. However, the supplied review does not verify a threonine-specific protocol with these concentrations, rates or yields. This corrected substrate-specific application and its environmental operating window remain provisional. Lactaldehyde hydration, oligomerization, reversible imine formation and competing cyanohydrin formation must be measured. No enantioselectivity is inferred."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Formation of the correctly sized lactaldehyde cyanohydrin", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_005"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydroxide from mildly alkaline porewater, acting as a general-base catalyst"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-310 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Hydroxide, general-base catalysis", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: pH 8.0-9.0; lactaldehyde 1-20 mM and uncomplexed HCN/CN- 10-100 mM; 1 hour to 3 days. Initially use ammonia-poor water to distinguish cyanohydrin storage from simultaneous Strecker chemistry. Maintain anoxia and local UV shielding. Determine the equilibrium cyanohydrin fraction and losses rather than assuming quantitative trapping."}, "mechanism": "Net: mol_001 + mol_002 -> mol_005. Cyanide adds to the aldehyde carbonyl, followed by proton transfer. The product is CH3-CH(OH)-CH(OH)-CN and contains four carbons.", "reasoning": "Carbonyl cyanohydrin formation supports this connectivity. The supplied attribution to Patel et al. (2015) contains a carbon-count error: a C3 aldehyde plus HCN cannot directly produce the listed C5 cyanohydrin. The corrected C4 reaction is chemically justified, but its substrate-specific equilibrium and geochemical yield are not established by the supplied evidence. Its proposed reservoir function is therefore speculative."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Cyanohydrin-reservoir conversion through Strecker equilibration", "inputs": ["mol_005", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_006", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-315 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: cyanohydrin 1-20 mM, total NH3/NH4+ 50-500 mM, pH 8.0-9.2; 1-14 days. Introduce ammonia-bearing water into the same sheltered surface porewater, accounting for dilution. Track lactaldehyde, cyanohydrin and aminonitrile separately. Do not proceed on the assumption that disappearance of cyanohydrin equals aminonitrile production."}, "mechanism": "Net: mol_005 + mol_003 -> mol_006 + mol_004. This is a composite equilibrium route: cyanohydrin dissociation releases lactaldehyde and HCN, lactaldehyde forms an imine with ammonia, and cyanide captures the imine/iminium species. It is not an elementary substitution of an unactivated hydroxyl group.", "reasoning": "This interpretation is consistent with Strecker chemistry (Strecker, 1850) and avoids the unsupported displacement and carbon-skeleton rearrangement described in the supplied Patel et al. (2015) summary. Productive reservoir-to-aminonitrile flux remains speculative without substrate-specific kinetic measurements. This entry history shares the microscopic Strecker chemistry of rxn_001 and is not independent evidence for a second reaction family."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Acid-assisted aminonitrile hydration to threoninamide", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_007"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydronium supplied by acidic volcanic condensate"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-370 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Hydronium", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: pH 0.5-1.5, aminonitrile 0.1-5 mM after mixing, and 30-300 mM free strong-acid capacity after neutralizing carried-over ammonia and alkalinity; sample over 6 hours to 30 days. Volcanic HCl-bearing condensate is an anoxia-compatible acid source, not oxygen-dependent sulfide weathering. Use shaded, water-filled hot-spring margins. An amide-rich fraction must be demonstrated before assigning a separate downstream processing stage."}, "mechanism": "Net: mol_006 + mol_004 -> mol_007. Proton-assisted water addition to the nitrile, followed by proton transfers and tautomerization, forms the carboxamide. Aminonitrile and amide formulas are neutral analytical reference forms; their reversible amino-group protonation is not expanded into additional nodes.", "reasoning": "Stepwise nitrile-to-amide-to-acid conversion belongs to the established Strecker hydrolysis family (Strecker, 1850). The supplied Patel et al. (2015) summary does not provide a verified threonine-aminonitrile hydrolysis protocol. These numerical conditions are consequently speculative screening conditions, not established operating conditions. Useful hydration must outrun retro-Strecker chemistry and decomposition; selective amide accumulation is not assumed."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Alkaline aminonitrile hydration to threoninamide", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_007"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydroxide supplied by hyperalkaline surface spring water"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-370 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Hydroxide, for the hydration step", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: free OH- 3-30 mM, corresponding approximately to pH 11.5-12.5 at 298 K; aminonitrile 0.1-5 mM after mixing; 6 hours to 30 days. Hot-fluid pH must be measured or calculated using temperature-dependent water ionization, not equated to the 298 K value. Determine available alkalinity after carbonate precipitation and acid carryover. Serpentinization-fed surface springs are a possible source, not a guaranteed reservoir."}, "mechanism": "Net: mol_006 + mol_004 -> mol_007. Hydroxide addition to the nitrile and subsequent proton transfers give the amide. Hydroxide may be regenerated during this hydration step; its net consumption during subsequent amide cleavage is explicitly represented in rxn_007.", "reasoning": "This is a chemically plausible but substrate-specific speculative alternative. The supplied Patel et al. (2015) account does not establish alkaline hydration rates, an amide yield, or product survival for this aminonitrile. The stated temperature and hydroxide concentrations are a test envelope only. Competing degradation and stereochemical scrambling must be quantified; a stronger base requirement would weaken the geochemical case."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Acidic threoninamide hydrolysis with explicit proton uptake", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_009"], "outputs": ["mol_012", "mol_011", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydronium as acid promoter; net proton uptake is included in the reaction"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-370 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Hydronium; acid promotion does not imply zero net acid consumption", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: pH 0.5-1.5, threoninamide 0.01-5 mM, 30-300 mM residual free strong-acid capacity; 1-30 days with time-resolved analysis. The displayed net reaction requires two acid equivalents per neutral amide, in addition to neutralizing other carried-over bases. For a preceding alkaline stage, cool and mix the entire intermediate-bearing solution with acidic condensate; include the resulting dilution. Move product-containing water to a 300-315 K zone within one hour after the selected residence interval."}, "mechanism": "Net: mol_007 + 2 mol_009 -> mol_012 + mol_011 + mol_004. Water attacks the proton-activated amide; C-N cleavage and proton transfers produce protonated threonine and ammonium. The two hydronium equivalents account for the displayed final ionic products.", "reasoning": "Amide hydrolysis is chemically well founded, but the supplied Patel et al. (2015) summary does not establish this operating window. Analytical amino-acid hydrolysis literature documents the need to account for hydrolysis-related losses (Fountoulakis and Lahm, 1998); it does not validate this prebiotic threoninamide conversion. This environmental application remains speculative. Threonine-spike degradation controls and independent product identification are required to establish net accumulation rather than merely amide disappearance."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Base-promoted threoninamide hydrolysis", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_010"], "outputs": ["mol_013", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "350-370 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: free OH- 3-30 mM, approximately pH 11.5-12.5 at 298 K, and threoninamide 0.01-5 mM; 1-30 days. Supply at least one net hydroxide equivalent per amide plus the separately measured demand from residual acid, ammonium and carbonate chemistry. For an acidic precursor stream, cool before mixing with alkaline spring water and calculate concentration after dilution. Terminate the hot exposure by transfer into adjacent cooler water; no selective mineral retention is assumed."}, "mechanism": "Net: mol_007 + mol_010 -> mol_013 + mol_003. Hydroxide attacks the amide carbonyl, and collapse of the tetrahedral intermediate produces carboxylate and ammonia. This is net base-consuming hydrolysis, not a catalytic hydroxide cycle.", "reasoning": "The net chemistry is standard amide hydrolysis, but no substrate-specific alkaline protocol is established in the supplied research; this environmental application is speculative. Patel et al. (2015) supplies broader amino-acid-precursor context rather than evidence for the stated rate or yield. Explicit hydroxide consumption corrects the alkalinity omission in earlier variants. The pathway fails if hydrolysis is too slow or degradation exceeds surviving threoninate production."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Neutralization of acidic threonine product", "inputs": ["mol_012", "mol_010"], "outputs": ["mol_008", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-315 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Mix cooled acidic product solution with anoxia-compatible alkaline surface water to a final pH of 5-7. The displayed substrate neutralization consumes one hydroxide equivalent per threoninium ion. Additional base must neutralize free acidity and other titratable species; determine that demand by a full titration balance. Mixing and proton equilibration are rapid relative to hydrolysis, but the actual residence and dilution depend on the physical site."}, "mechanism": "Net: mol_012 + mol_010 -> mol_008 + mol_004. Deprotonation of the carboxylic acid gives the electrically neutral, predominantly zwitterionic amino-acid pool. This changes protonation state, not carbon connectivity or stereochemistry.", "reasoning": "Proton transfer is established acid-base chemistry, not an additional amino-acid synthesis mechanism. Its particular geochemical implementation is speculative and is not documented by the supplied Patel et al. (2015) summary. This node makes acidic product speciation and the minimum neutralization demand explicit; it does not imply crystallization, purification or chiral resolution."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Neutralization of alkaline threonine product", "inputs": ["mol_013", "mol_009"], "outputs": ["mol_008", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-315 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Mix cooled alkaline product water with acidic volcanic condensate to a final pH of 5-7. The displayed target transformation consumes one hydronium equivalent per threoninate. The total acid demand also includes remaining hydroxide, ammonia protonation and carbonate neutralization; allow approximately one additional equivalent per retained NH3 and up to two per carbonate when it is converted to dissolved CO2. Report final concentrations after mixing rather than assuming quantitative concentration or retention."}, "mechanism": "Net: mol_013 + mol_009 -> mol_008 + mol_004. Protonation of the amino group gives the predominantly zwitterionic target pool. Other solution-buffer neutralizations contribute to the bulk acid budget but are not additional target-forming transformations.", "reasoning": "The proton-transfer chemistry is established; the environmental mixing history is speculative and is not a reported threonine protocol in Patel et al. (2015). Explicitly accounting for the target protonation step avoids treating neutral threonine as the immediate dominant species in strongly alkaline hydrolysis. It does not solve the preceding kinetic bottleneck."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Candidate Bucherer-Bergs hydantoin trapping", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_014"], "outputs": ["mol_015"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "320-350 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: aminonitrile 1-10 mM, total dissolved inorganic carbon 20-100 mM, total NH3/NH4+ 20-100 mM and pH 8.0-9.2; 1-14 days. Distinguish total inorganic carbon from free dissolved CO2. Use anoxic, UV-shielded carbonate/ammonium-bearing porewater. Identify the substituted hydantoin directly; precipitation, protection and quantitative trapping are not assumed."}, "mechanism": "Net: mol_006 + mol_014 -> mol_015. Aminonitrile carbamylation, intramolecular cyclization and rearrangement can assemble the hydantoin ring. The net transformation incorporates CO2 without net water consumption; it is not a single elementary collision.", "reasoning": "Bucherer-Bergs chemistry supplies a genuine alternative reaction family for carbonyl-derived aminonitrile trapping (Bucherer and Lieb, 1934). Neither that general precedent nor the supplied Patel et al. (2015) summary establishes this beta-hydroxy substrate under the stated conditions. This branch is speculative and is included specifically to test whether a persistent precursor reservoir can mitigate aminonitrile loss. Any protective benefit and the eventual recoverability of threonine must be demonstrated."}, {"id": "rxn_011", "name": "Candidate alkaline hydantoin ring opening", "inputs": ["mol_015", "mol_010"], "outputs": ["mol_016"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "360-370 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed lower-temperature screen: hydantoin 0.01-1 mM after fluid mixing, free OH- 3-30 mM, approximately pH 11.5-12.5 at 298 K; 1-90 days. Introduce alkaline spring water into the same surface setting rather than invoking deep-vent transport. Quantify the alkalinity consumed by the original ammonium/carbonate medium and dilution during replacement. Require direct identification of N-carbamoylthreoninate; hotter or more concentrated preparative conditions are not silently substituted."}, "mechanism": "Net: mol_015 + mol_010 -> mol_016. Hydroxide-promoted acyl cleavage opens the hydantoin ring to the N-carbamoyl amino-acid carboxylate. One hydroxide equivalent is consumed in the displayed neutral-hydantoin reference equation.", "reasoning": "Hydantoin-to-carbamoyl-amino-acid chemistry is consistent with the broader Bucherer-Bergs framework (Bucherer and Lieb, 1934), but the supplied research does not establish this substrate-specific surface reaction. The stated screen is speculative and may fail because useful ring opening can require harsher conditions. No useful residence time, protective effect or product survival is claimed in advance."}, {"id": "rxn_012", "name": "Candidate alkaline decarbamoylation to threoninate", "inputs": ["mol_016", "mol_010"], "outputs": ["mol_013", "mol_003", "mol_017"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "360-370 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed feasibility screen only: N-carbamoylthreoninate 0.01-1 mM, free OH- 3-30 mM, approximately pH 11.5-12.5 at 298 K; 1-90 days. Budget two additional hydroxide equivalents per substrate when the released inorganic carbon is represented as carbonate. Monitor substrate, threoninate, ammonia, inorganic carbon and degradation products. A threonine-spike survival control is essential. Reject this branch if net target accumulation cannot be shown within the permitted window."}, "mechanism": "Net: mol_016 + 2 mol_010 -> mol_013 + mol_003 + mol_017. Hydrolysis of the carbamoyl group releases the amino acid and ammonia; alkaline capture of the removed carbonyl carbon as carbonate gives the displayed net hydroxide demand.", "reasoning": "Speculative and the weakest target-releasing step in this network: the supplied research contains no verified nonenzymatic protocol for this substrate at these temperatures and alkalinities. Generic hydantoin chemistry is not sufficient evidence that the carbamoyl group can be removed before beta-hydroxy amino-acid degradation. The relatively resistant carbamoyl linkage may make this branch inoperative in surface waters. P9 and P10 therefore remain conditional alternatives, not validated prebiotic routes."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "Direct Strecker entry with continuously acidic hydrolysis", "description": "Conditional reference pathway: lactaldehyde undergoes Strecker assembly, followed by nitrile hydration and amide cleavage in acidic water, then cooling and neutralization. No isolation of the amide is required. Productive aminonitrile formation and net threonine survival remain unverified.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_004", "rxn_006", "rxn_008"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_006", "mol_007", "mol_012"]}, {"pathway_id": "P2", "name": "Direct Strecker entry with continuously alkaline hydrolysis", "description": "Conditional alkaline alternative. Nitrile hydration is followed by base-consuming amide cleavage and final protonation. Available alkalinity must be demonstrated after buffering and precipitation losses; no rate advantage over P1 is asserted.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_005", "rxn_007", "rxn_009"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_006", "mol_007", "mol_013"]}, {"pathway_id": "P3", "name": "Direct Strecker entry with acidic hydration and alkaline cleavage", "description": "Conditional split-stage history testing whether the preferred operating windows for nitrile hydration and amide cleavage differ. Requires a measured amide inventory, cooling, neutralization of the acidic liquor and mixing with sufficient alkaline water. Dilution is carried forward; improved yield is not assumed.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_004", "rxn_007", "rxn_009"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_006", "mol_007", "mol_013"]}, {"pathway_id": "P4", "name": "Direct Strecker entry with alkaline hydration and acidic cleavage", "description": "Conditional reverse split-stage history. An experimentally demonstrated amide-containing alkaline solution is cooled and mixed with acidic condensate before amide cleavage. Acid supply must exceed the complete carried-over alkalinity and ammonia demand.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_008"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_006", "mol_007", "mol_012"]}, {"pathway_id": "P5", "name": "Cyanohydrin-reservoir entry with acidic hydrolysis", "description": "Cyanohydrin formation precedes ammonia delivery, followed by reversible release and Strecker trapping. The aminonitrile then follows acidic hydration and cleavage. This is distinct as a storage and feedstock-delivery history, not as an independent microscopic Strecker mechanism.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_006", "rxn_008"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_005", "mol_006", "mol_007", "mol_012"]}, {"pathway_id": "P6", "name": "Cyanohydrin-reservoir entry with alkaline hydrolysis", "description": "Conditional reservoir-first entry followed by alkaline nitrile and amide processing. Residual cyanohydrin must be distinguished from aminonitrile because its hydrolysis produces non-amino products. The base and product-survival requirements of P2 also apply.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_005", "rxn_007", "rxn_009"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_005", "mol_006", "mol_007", "mol_013"]}, {"pathway_id": "P7", "name": "Cyanohydrin-reservoir entry with acidic hydration and alkaline cleavage", "description": "Conditional combination of reservoir release and acid-to-base processing. Requires measured conversion to aminonitrile and then a persistent amide pool before the pH change. Neutralization, dilution and substrate recovery must be quantified; pathway enumeration is not additional experimental evidence.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_007", "rxn_009"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_005", "mol_006", "mol_007", "mol_013"]}, {"pathway_id": "P8", "name": "Cyanohydrin-reservoir entry with alkaline hydration and acidic cleavage", "description": "Conditional reservoir-first route with a base-to-acid processing transition. The same surface solution carries its organic intermediates between stages; no selective mineral adsorption or unexplained cross-environment transport is assumed. Its performance relative to continuous-pH processing is unknown.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_008"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_005", "mol_006", "mol_007", "mol_012"]}, {"pathway_id": "P9", "name": "Direct Strecker entry with hydantoin trapping and release", "description": "Lower-evidence candidate: aminonitrile is trapped as a hydantoin, opened to N-carbamoylthreoninate and subjected to decarbamoylation before neutralization. This tests a chemically distinct reservoir hypothesis. It cannot be considered operational unless rxn_011 and especially rxn_012 release surviving target under the stated constraints.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_010", "rxn_011", "rxn_012", "rxn_009"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_006", "mol_015", "mol_016", "mol_013"]}, {"pathway_id": "P10", "name": "Cyanohydrin-first entry with hydantoin trapping and release", "description": "Lower-evidence candidate combining two sequential storage hypotheses: a cyanohydrin reservoir followed by hydantoin trapping. It has the additional dependency of productive cyanohydrin-to-aminonitrile transfer and shares the unresolved carbamoyl-release bottleneck of P9. No protective or kinetic superiority is claimed.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_010", "rxn_011", "rxn_012", "rxn_009"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_005", "mol_006", "mol_015", "mol_016", "mol_013"]}], "hub_molecules": ["mol_001", "mol_006", "mol_007", "mol_008", "mol_013"], "convergence_points": [{"molecule_id": "mol_006", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_001", "rxn_003"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_010"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_007", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_004", "rxn_005"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_006", "rxn_007"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_013", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_007", "rxn_012"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_009"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_008", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_008", "rxn_009"], "outgoing_reactions": []}]}, "reasoning": "", "duration_ms": 600958.801984787, "success": true, "error_message": null}
{"timestamp": "2026-10-19T15:33:02.125184", "agent_name": "CriticAgent", "method_name": "evaluate_network", "step": null, "attempt": null, "system_message": "You are evaluating a COMPLETE SYNTHESIS NETWORK, not individual reactions.\n\n## Neutrality\n- Do not reward or penalize a network for using (or not using) any particular feedstock, intermediate,\n  reaction family, or prebiotic framework. Judge only the evidence and the chemistry.\n- The number of shared/hub molecules, the number of convergence points, and the order or number of\n  environments used are NOT scoring criteria.\n\n## Evaluation Criteria\n\n### 1. Network Validity (0.0-1.0)\n- Are there sufficient molecule and reaction nodes?\n- Is the network connected (no isolated subgraphs)?\n- Are molecule IDs used consistently, and are reactions approximately balanced?\n- When a DETERMINISTIC ATOM/CHARGE BALANCE CHECK section is provided, treat it as ground truth for\n  mass and charge conservation; reactions it reports as UNBALANCED are conservation violations.\n\n### 2. Chemical Feasibility (0.0-1.0)\n- Is each reaction chemically sound?\n- Are the conditions appropriate for each environment?\n- Are the mechanisms plausible?\n\n### 3. Environmental Consistency (0.0-1.0)\n- Are each reaction's conditions (temperature, pressure, pH, catalysts, energy source) consistent with its stated environment?\n- If molecules are transferred between environments, is the transfer physically justified?\n- If a catalyst or mineral agent is cited, is it realistic and present in that environment?\n\n### 4. Pathway Coherence (0.0-1.0)\n- Do pathways form valid routes to the target?\n- Are key intermediates properly identified?\n- Is the overall strategy sound?\n\n### 5. Target Achievement (0.0-1.0)\n- Does the network actually produce the target molecule?\n- Are the final steps chemically valid?\n\n### 6. Literature Alignment (0.0-1.0) - When research findings are provided\n- Are reactions supported by experimental evidence from cited studies?\n- Are literature citations present in reaction reasoning?\n- Does the network acknowledge critiques and known challenges identified in the research?\n- Departures from the research are acceptable when explicitly and soundly justified.\n\n## Scoring Guidelines\n- 0.9-1.0: Excellent network, valid and chemically sound\n- 0.8-0.9: Good network with minor issues\n- 0.7-0.8: Acceptable but with notable concerns\n- 0.5-0.7: Significant problems with validity or chemistry\n- 0.0-0.5: Major issues, network is not viable\n\nA network is APPROVED if overall_confidence >= 0.9\n\nOutput your evaluation as JSON matching the NetworkEvaluation schema.\n", "user_message": "Evaluate this synthesis network:\n\nTARGET MOLECULE: Threonine\nOVERALL STRATEGY: An evidence-qualified network containing 17 molecule nodes, 12 reaction nodes and 10 distinct reaction-sequence pathways. The ten pathways are candidate processing histories, not ten experimentally demonstrated syntheses: two Strecker entry histories combine with four acid/base hydrolysis histories, while two additional pathways examine hydantoin trapping and release. The supplied review does not establish ten independent routes or provide reaction-specific operating protocols. Its lactaldehyde cyanohydrin must be C4H7NO2, not C5H9NO2; cyanohydrin conversion is modeled through reversible aldehyde release and imine capture, not direct hydroxyl displacement. Alanine plus formaldehyde is excluded because ordinary alpha-carbon addition gives the wrong connectivity. Lactaldehyde is an explicit external C3 feedstock; its geochemical supply remains unresolved rather than being filled by an unsupported upstream reaction. All numerical conditions below are proposed experimental screening windows, not reported successful protocols or predicted yields. All stages use anoxic surface waters or shallow, UV-shielded porewaters. Acid and alkaline fluids are supplied separately; mixing, neutralization and dilution cannot be ignored. The target is connectivity-defined: neither L-selectivity nor separation of threonine from allothreonine is established. Molecular equations describe net transformations, not exhaustive aqueous speciation. Spectator counterions are omitted, but net charge and atom conservation are maintained. Organic branching and convergence nodes are identified separately from ubiquitous water and acid/base reagents.\n\nMOLECULES (17 nodes):\n  - mol_001: C3H6O2 (Lactaldehyde) [starting]\n  - mol_002: CHN (Hydrogen cyanide) [starting]\n  - mol_003: NH3 (Ammonia) [starting]\n  - mol_004: H2O (Water) [starting]\n  - mol_005: C4H7NO2 (Lactaldehyde cyanohydrin) [intermediate]\n  - mol_006: C4H8N2O (Threonine aminonitrile) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_007: C4H10N2O2 (Threoninamide) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_008: C4H9NO3 (Threonine, stereochemistry unspecified) [target]\n  - mol_009: H3O+ (Hydronium) [starting]\n  - mol_010: OH- (Hydroxide) [starting]\n  - mol_011: NH4+ (Ammonium) [intermediate]\n  - mol_012: C4H10NO3+ (Threoninium) [intermediate]\n  - mol_013: C4H8NO3- (Threoninate) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_014: CO2 (Carbon dioxide) [starting]\n  - mol_015: C5H8N2O3 (Threonine hydantoin) [intermediate]\n  - mol_016: C5H9N2O4- (N-carbamoylthreoninate) [intermediate]\n  - mol_017: CO3^2- (Carbonate) [intermediate]\n\nREACTIONS (12 nodes):\n  - rxn_001: mol_001, mol_002, mol_003 -> mol_006, mol_004 (Direct lactaldehyde Strecker assembly) [Surface]\n  - rxn_002: mol_001, mol_002 -> mol_005 (Formation of the correctly sized lactaldehyde cyanohydrin) [Surface]\n  - rxn_003: mol_005, mol_003 -> mol_006, mol_004 (Cyanohydrin-reservoir conversion through Strecker equilibration) [Surface]\n  - rxn_004: mol_006, mol_004 -> mol_007 (Acid-assisted aminonitrile hydration to threoninamide) [Surface]\n  - rxn_005: mol_006, mol_004 -> mol_007 (Alkaline aminonitrile hydration to threoninamide) [Surface]\n  - rxn_006: mol_007, mol_009 -> mol_012, mol_011, mol_004 (Acidic threoninamide hydrolysis with explicit proton uptake) [Surface]\n  - rxn_007: mol_007, mol_010 -> mol_013, mol_003 (Base-promoted threoninamide hydrolysis) [Surface]\n  - rxn_008: mol_012, mol_010 -> mol_008, mol_004 (Neutralization of acidic threonine product) [Surface]\n  - rxn_009: mol_013, mol_009 -> mol_008, mol_004 (Neutralization of alkaline threonine product) [Surface]\n  - rxn_010: mol_006, mol_014 -> mol_015 (Candidate Bucherer-Bergs hydantoin trapping) [Surface]\n  - rxn_011: mol_015, mol_010 -> mol_016 (Candidate alkaline hydantoin ring opening) [Surface]\n  - rxn_012: mol_016, mol_010 -> mol_013, mol_003, mol_017 (Candidate alkaline decarbamoylation to threoninate) [Surface]\n\nPATHWAYS (10):\n  - P1: Direct Strecker entry with continuously acidic hydrolysis (4 steps)\n  - P2: Direct Strecker entry with continuously alkaline hydrolysis (4 steps)\n  - P3: Direct Strecker entry with acidic hydration and alkaline cleavage (4 steps)\n  - P4: Direct Strecker entry with alkaline hydration and acidic cleavage (4 steps)\n  - P5: Cyanohydrin-reservoir entry with acidic hydrolysis (5 steps)\n  - P6: Cyanohydrin-reservoir entry with alkaline hydrolysis (5 steps)\n  - P7: Cyanohydrin-reservoir entry with acidic hydration and alkaline cleavage (5 steps)\n  - P8: Cyanohydrin-reservoir entry with alkaline hydration and acidic cleavage (5 steps)\n  - P9: Direct Strecker entry with hydantoin trapping and release (5 steps)\n  - P10: Cyanohydrin-first entry with hydantoin trapping and release (6 steps)\n\n\n=== DETERMINISTIC ATOM/CHARGE BALANCE CHECK (RDKit) ===\nComputed programmatically from each molecule's InChI (via RDKit) or stated formula. Reactions list no coefficients, so a reaction counts as balanced if some positive integer coefficients (<= 24) conserve every element and net charge.\nSummary: 12 reactions | 10 balanced as written | 2 balanceable with coefficients | 0 UNBALANCED | 0 unchecked\n  - rxn_006 balanceable: C4H10N2O2 + 2 H3O+ -> C4H10NO3+ + NH4+ + H2O\n  - rxn_012 balanceable: C5H9N2O4- + 2 OH- -> C4H8NO3- + NH3 + CO3^2-\nFormula/structure inconsistencies:\n  - mol_014: InChI could not be parsed by RDKit (RDKit could not parse InChI)\nTreat this check as ground truth for mass and charge conservation. Reactions that cannot be balanced violate conservation: reflect this in chemical_feasibility and reaction_scores, list them in problematic_reactions, and name the specific reaction IDs (with the missing species, if hinted) in your feedback so the network can be corrected. Note that implausibly large inferred coefficients may also indicate a mis-specified reaction.\n=== END BALANCE CHECK ===\n\n\n=== LITERATURE RESEARCH ON TARGET MOLECULE ===\nThe following research was conducted on the target molecule before network generation. Use it to assess whether the network aligns with known prebiotic chemistry literature.\n\n=== CURATED LITERATURE REVIEW ===\n# Prebiotic Synthesis of L-Threonine: Comprehensive Literature Review\n\n## 1. Known Prebiotic Synthesis Routes\n\nTwo experimentally demonstrated pathways produce L-threonine (C4H9NO3, SMILES: CC(C(C(=O)O)N)O, InChI: InChI=1S/C4H9NO3/c1-2(6)3(5)4(7)8/h2-3,6H,5H2,1H3,(H,7,8), MW: 119.12) from prebiotically available precursors. These span surface and hydrothermal environments.\n\n### Pathway 1: Cyanosulfidic Nitrile Hydrolysis via Lactaldehyde Cyanohydrin (Surface Environment)\n\nThe cyanosulfidic protometabolism pathway demonstrated by Patel, Percivalle, Ritson, Duffy, and Sutherland (2015, *Nature Chemistry*, DOI: 10.1038/nchem.2202) constructs L-threonine through a 3-step sequence starting from **lactaldehyde** and **hydrogen cyanide**, proceeding through a cyanohydrin intermediate and an aminonitrile intermediate. This pathway follows the general cyanosulfidic strategy: an aldehyde undergoes cyanohydrin formation with HCN, amination with ammonia displaces the hydroxyl to form an aminonitrile, and final nitrile hydrolysis yields the amino acid.\n\n**Step 1**: Lactaldehyde + HCN \u2192 Cyanohydrin intermediate (Cyanohydrin formation)\n- SMILES: CC(O)C=O.C#N>>CC(O)CC(O)C#N\n- Reactants: Lactaldehyde (C3H6O2, SMILES: CC(C=O)O, InChI: InChI=1S/C3H6O2/c1-3(5)2-4/h2-3,5H,1H3, MW: 74.08) + Hydrogen cyanide (CHN, SMILES: C#N, InChI: InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H, MW: 27.03)\n- Product: Cyanohydrin intermediate (C5H9NO2, SMILES: CC(O)CC(O)C#N, InChI: InChI=1S/C5H9NO2/c1-4(7)2-5(8)3-6/h4-5,7-8H,2H2,1H3, MW: 115.06)\n- Environment: Surface (evaporitic pools, cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Conditions: Aqueous, ambient temperature and pressure\n- Mechanism: Nucleophilic addition of cyanide (CN\u207b) to the aldehyde carbonyl carbon of lactaldehyde. The cyanide ion attacks the electrophilic carbonyl carbon of the aldehyde, and subsequent protonation of the resulting alkoxide yields the cyanohydrin. This is a classic Kiliani-type cyanohydrin reaction that extends the carbon chain by one carbon unit while introducing a nitrile functional group. Lactaldehyde is a plausible prebiotic C3 aldol product available from cyanosulfidic chemistry.\n\n**Step 2**: Cyanohydrin intermediate + NH3 \u2192 2-Amino-3-hydroxybutanenitrile + H2O (Aminonitrile formation)\n- SMILES: CC(O)CC(O)C#N.N>>CC(O)C(N)C#N.O\n- Reactants: Cyanohydrin intermediate (C5H9NO2, SMILES: CC(O)CC(O)C#N) + Ammonia (H3N, SMILES: N, InChI: InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3, MW: 17.03)\n- Products: 2-Amino-3-hydroxybutanenitrile (C4H8N2O, SMILES: CC(C(C#N)N)O, InChI: InChI=1S/C4H8N2O/c1-3(7)4(6)2-5/h3-4,7H,6H2,1H3, MW: 100.12) + H2O (SMILES: O)\n- Environment: Surface (evaporitic pools, cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Conditions: Aqueous, ambient temperature and pressure\n- Mechanism: Strecker-type amination \u2014 ammonia displaces the hydroxyl group at the alpha-carbon adjacent to the nitrile, converting the cyanohydrin to an aminonitrile with release of water. The reaction involves nucleophilic attack of NH3 on the carbon bearing the hydroxyl group, with rearrangement of the carbon skeleton to yield the C4 aminonitrile with the beta-hydroxy group characteristic of threonine.\n\n**Step 3**: 2-Amino-3-hydroxybutanenitrile + 2 H2O \u2192 L-Threonine + NH3 (Nitrile hydrolysis)\n- SMILES: CC(O)C(N)C#N.O.O>>CC(O)C(N)C(=O)O.N\n- Reactants: 2-Amino-3-hydroxybutanenitrile (C4H8N2O, SMILES: CC(C(C#N)N)O) + 2 H2O (SMILES: O)\n- Products: L-Threonine (C4H9NO3, SMILES: CC(C(C(=O)O)N)O, InChI: InChI=1S/C4H9NO3/c1-2(6)3(5)4(7)8/h2-3,6H,5H2,1H3,(H,7,8), MW: 119.12) + Ammonia (H3N, SMILES: N, InChI: InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3)\n- Environment: Surface (evaporitic pools, cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Conditions: Aqueous, ambient temperature and pressure\n- Mechanism: Nucleophilic addition of water to the nitrile group of 2-amino-3-hydroxybutanenitrile, forming an amide intermediate (2-amino-3-hydroxybutanamide), followed by further hydrolysis to the carboxylic acid (threonine) with release of ammonia (NH3). This is the final step of the general cyanosulfidic aminonitrile-to-amino acid pathway, converting the C\u2261N triple bond to a carboxylic acid via stepwise hydration.\n\nThis three-step sequence is part of the broader Powell-Sutherland cyanosulfidic chemistry framework, in which HCN-derived nitriles serve as amino acid precursors via hydrolysis under wet-dry cycling or UV-irradiated surface pool conditions. The same paper demonstrates that this unified chemistry produces precursors to RNA, proteins, and lipids from simple feedstocks. The threonine pathway parallels the serine pathway (glycolaldehyde + HCN) but uses lactaldehyde as the starting aldehyde, introducing the methyl branch that distinguishes threonine from serine.\n\n### Pathway 2: Aldol Condensation of L-Alanine with Formaldehyde (Hydrothermal Environment)\n\nAubrey, Cleaves, and Bada (2008, *Origins of Life and Evolution of Biospheres*, DOI: 10.1007/s11084-008-9153-2) demonstrated that **L-alanine** (C3H7NO2, SMILES: CC(C(=O)O)N, InChI: InChI=1S/C3H7NO2/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h2H,4H2,1H3,(H,5,6), MW: 89.09) reacts with **formaldehyde** (CH2O, SMILES: C=O, InChI: InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2, MW: 30.03) to produce L-threonine under submarine hydrothermal conditions.\n\n**Reaction**: L-Alanine + Formaldehyde \u2192 L-Threonine\n- SMILES: C[C@H](N)C(=O)O.C=O>>C[C@@H](O)[C@H](N)C(=O)O\n- Environment: Hydrothermal (submarine hydrothermal systems)\n- Conditions: Elevated temperature and pressure characteristic of submarine hydrothermal vents\n- Mechanism: Aldol-type condensation \u2014 the alpha-carbon of L-alanine attacks the electrophilic carbonyl carbon of formaldehyde, forming a new C\u2013C bond and introducing a hydroxymethyl group (-CH(OH)-). This converts the C3 amino acid (alanine) into the C4 amino acid (threonine) by extending the carbon skeleton by one carbon unit. Formaldehyde is a key C1 electrophile that is abundantly available in hydrothermal vent fluids from reduction of CO2 and Fischer-Tropsch-type chemistry.\n\nThis pathway links threonine synthesis to L-alanine, which is itself produced by multiple prebiotic routes including: (a) reductive amination of pyruvate with hydroxylamine in the presence of iron (Muchowska et al., 2019), (b) hydrolysis of 2-aminopropanenitrile under cyanosulfidic conditions (Patel et al., 2015), and (c) reduction of L-serine under hydrothermal conditions (Aubrey et al., 2008). The reaction is directly analogous to the glycine + formaldehyde \u2192 serine aldol condensation demonstrated by the same research group, but applied one carbon unit higher in the amino acid homologous series.\n\n## 2. Key Intermediates and Precursors\n\n### Hub Molecule: L-Alanine (C3H7NO2)\n- Formula: C3H7NO2\n- SMILES: CC(C(=O)O)N\n- InChI: InChI=1S/C3H7NO2/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h2H,4H2,1H3,(H,5,6)\n- MW: 89.09\n- Role: **Key amino acid precursor for Pathway 2** \u2014 L-alanine is the substrate for the aldol condensation with formaldehyde that produces threonine (Aubrey et al., 2008). Alanine is itself produced by multiple prebiotic routes: reductive amination of pyruvate with hydroxylamine using iron catalysts at 70\u00b0C (Muchowska et al., 2019), hydrolysis of 2-aminopropanenitrile under cyanosulfidic conditions (Patel et al., 2015), and reduction of L-serine under hydrothermal conditions (Aubrey et al., 2008). Alanine is one of the most abundant amino acids produced in prebiotic chemistry experiments.\n\n### Key Intermediate: Cyanohydrin Intermediate (C5H9NO2)\n- Formula: C5H9NO2\n- SMILES: CC(O)CC(O)C#N\n- InChI: InChI=1S/C5H9NO2/c1-4(7)2-5(8)3-6/h4-5,7-8H,2H2,1H3\n- MW: 115.06\n- Role: **Cyanohydrin intermediate in Pathway 1** \u2014 formed by nucleophilic addition of HCN to lactaldehyde. This intermediate is the direct precursor to 2-amino-3-hydroxybutanenitrile via amination with ammonia. Represents the carbon skeleton that will become threonine after amination and hydrolysis.\n\n### Key Intermediate: 2-Amino-3-hydroxybutanenitrile (C4H8N2O)\n- Formula: C4H8N2O\n- SMILES: CC(C(C#N)N)O\n- InChI: InChI=1S/C4H8N2O/c1-3(7)4(6)2-5/h3-4,7H,6H2,1H3\n- MW: 100.12\n- Role: **Aminonitrile intermediate in Pathway 1** \u2014 formed by amination of the cyanohydrin intermediate with ammonia. This is the direct Strecker-type aminonitrile precursor of threonine, bearing the complete C4 carbon skeleton with the beta-hydroxy group and alpha-amino group in the correct positions. Hydrolysis of the nitrile group yields threonine.\n\n### Other Key Molecules\n\n**Lactaldehyde** (C3H6O2)\n- Formula: C3H6O2\n- SMILES: CC(C=O)O\n- InChI: InChI=1S/C3H6O2/c1-3(5)2-4/h2-3,5H,1H3\n- MW: 74.08\n- Role: Starting aldehyde for Pathway 1 \u2014 undergoes cyanohydrin formation with HCN to initiate the cyanosulfidic threonine synthesis. Lactaldehyde is a C3 hydroxy aldehyde that is prebiotically plausible as a product of aldol chemistry and sugar photochemistry under cyanosulfidic conditions.\n\n**Hydrogen Cyanide** (CHN)\n- Formula: CHN\n- SMILES: C#N\n- InChI: InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H\n- MW: 27.03\n- Role: Key C1 nucleophile for cyanohydrin formation in Pathway 1. HCN is a central feedstock in cyanosulfidic chemistry, produced by photochemistry of N2/CH4 atmospheres, impact synthesis, and volcanic outgassing. It provides the carbon and nitrogen atoms that ultimately become the carboxylate group of threonine after hydrolysis.\n\n**Formaldehyde** (CH2O)\n- Formula: CH2O\n- SMILES: C=O\n- InChI: InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2\n- MW: 30.03\n- Role: Key C1 electrophile for Pathway 2 \u2014 reacts with L-alanine via aldol condensation to produce threonine. Formaldehyde is a common prebiotic starting molecule produced by photoreduction of CO2, hydrothermal reduction of CO2, Fischer-Tropsch-type chemistry on mineral surfaces, and UV photolysis of methane.\n\n### Starting Molecules\n- **H2O**: SMILES: O, InChI: InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2\n- **NH3** (ammonia): SMILES: N, InChI: InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3\n- **HCN** (hydrogen cyanide): SMILES: C#N, InChI: InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H\n- **H2CO** (formaldehyde): SMILES: C=O, InChI: InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2\n\n## 3. Reaction Mechanisms and Conditions\n\n### Reactions Producing Intermediates (Upstream)\n\n**Reaction U1: HCN cyanohydrin addition to lactaldehyde**\n- Lactaldehyde + HCN \u2192 Cyanohydrin intermediate\n- SMILES: CC(O)C=O.C#N>>CC(O)CC(O)C#N\n- Environment: Surface (cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Conditions: Aqueous, ambient temperature and pressure\n- Source: Patel, Percivalle, Ritson, Duffy, and Sutherland (2015), *Nature Chemistry*, DOI: 10.1038/nchem.2202\n- Mechanism: Nucleophilic addition of cyanide to the aldehyde carbonyl of lactaldehyde, forming a cyanohydrin. This is a classic Kiliani reaction that extends the carbon chain by one unit. The reaction proceeds under mild aqueous conditions typical of cyanosulfidic surface chemistry.\n\n**Reaction U2: Aminonitrile formation from cyanohydrin + ammonia**\n- Cyanohydrin intermediate + NH3 \u2192 2-Amino-3-hydroxybutanenitrile + H2O\n- SMILES: CC(O)CC(O)C#N.N>>CC(O)C(N)C#N.O\n- Environment: Surface (cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Conditions: Aqueous, ambient temperature and pressure\n- Source: Patel, Percivalle, Ritson, Duffy, and Sutherland (2015), *Nature Chemistry*, DOI: 10.1038/nchem.2202\n- Mechanism: Strecker-type amination \u2014 ammonia displaces the hydroxyl group at the alpha-carbon adjacent to the nitrile, converting the cyanohydrin to an aminonitrile with release of water. The reaction involves nucleophilic attack of NH3 on the carbon bearing the hydroxyl group, with rearrangement of the carbon skeleton to yield the C4 aminonitrile with the beta-hydroxy group characteristic of threonine.\n\n### Reactions Producing L-Threonine (Final Steps)\n\n**Reaction F1: Nitrile hydrolysis of 2-amino-3-hydroxybutanenitrile**\n- 2-Amino-3-hydroxybutanenitrile + 2 H2O \u2192 L-Threonine + NH3\n- SMILES: CC(O)C(N)C#N.O.O>>CC(O)C(N)C(=O)O.N\n- Environment: Surface (cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Conditions: Aqueous, ambient temperature and pressure\n- Source: Patel, Percivalle, Ritson, Duffy, and Sutherland (2015), *Nature Chemistry*, DOI: 10.1038/nchem.2202\n- Mechanism: Stepwise nitrile hydrolysis \u2014 water attacks the nitrile carbon, forming an amide intermediate (2-amino-3-hydroxybutanamide), which further hydrolyzes to the carboxylic acid (threonine) with release of ammonia. This is the terminal step of the general Strecker/cyanosulfidic aminonitrile-to-amino acid conversion.\n\n**Reaction F2: Aldol condensation of L-alanine with formaldehyde**\n- L-Alanine + Formaldehyde \u2192 L-Threonine\n- SMILES: C[C@H](N)C(=O)O.C=O>>C[C@@H](O)[C@H](N)C(=O)O\n- Environment: Hydrothermal (submarine hydrothermal systems)\n- Conditions: Elevated temperature and pressure\n- Source: Aubrey, Cleaves, and Bada (2008), *Origins of Life and Evolution of Biospheres*, DOI: 10.1007/s11084-008-9153-2\n- Mechanism: Aldol condensation \u2014 the alpha-carbon of L-alanine attacks the electrophilic carbonyl carbon of formaldehyde, forming a new C\u2013C bond and introducing a beta-hydroxyl group. This extends the C3 amino acid (alanine) to the C4 amino acid (threonine). The reaction is analogous to the glycine + formaldehyde \u2192 serine aldol condensation from the same study.\n\n## 4. Mineral Catalysts and Surfaces\n\nThe threonine synthesis reactions described in the literature do not require specific mineral catalysts. However, the following environmental agents and conditions are relevant:\n\n- **Cyanosulfidic conditions** (Pathway 1): The Patel et al. (2015) reactions operate under the cyanosulfidic chemistry framework, which involves hydrogen cyanide (HCN), hydrogen sulfide (H2S), and UV irradiation in surface pools. While no specific mineral catalysts are documented for the threonine-specific reactions, the broader cyanosulfidic network utilizes phosphate minerals for phosphorylation and UV light as an energy source.\n\n- **Hydrothermal mineral surfaces** (Pathway 2): The Aubrey et al. (2008) aldol condensation operates under submarine hydrothermal conditions. While no specific mineral catalyst is documented for the alanine-formaldehyde condensation itself, the hydrothermal environment provides elevated temperatures and mineral surfaces (e.g., iron-sulfide minerals, clay minerals) that may facilitate the reaction. Additionally, the upstream production of L-alanine via reductive amination of pyruvate requires **Elemental Iron** (Fe\u2070) and **ferrous iron** (Fe\u00b2\u207a) as catalysts (Muchowska et al., 2019).\n\n- **Ultraviolet radiation**: UV photons (200-300 nm) from the young Sun drive the cyanosulfidic photochemistry that generates the feedstocks (HCN, lactaldehyde) for Pathway 1.\n\n## 5. Experimental Evidence\n\n### Study 1: Patel et al. (2015)\n- **Title**: \"Common origins of RNA, protein and lipid precursors in a cyanosulfidic protometabolism\"\n- **Journal**: Nature Chemistry\n- **DOI**: 10.1038/nchem.2202\n- **Key finding**: Demonstrated that a unified cyanosulfidic chemistry starting from HCN and H2S can produce precursors to RNA, proteins, and lipids. For threonine specifically, the paper describes a three-step pathway: (1) HCN cyanohydrin addition to lactaldehyde produces a cyanohydrin intermediate (C5H9NO2), (2) amination with ammonia converts the cyanohydrin to 2-amino-3-hydroxybutanenitrile (C4H8N2O), and (3) nitrile hydrolysis yields L-threonine with release of ammonia. This is the cyanosulfidic analog of the Strecker synthesis, where the starting aldehyde (lactaldehyde) determines the amino acid product (threonine). The same framework produces glycine (from formaldehyde), alanine (from acetaldehyde), serine (from glycolaldehyde), and valine (from dihydroxyacetone-derived acetone), demonstrating a unified chemical origin for the amino acid repertoire.\n\n### Study 2: Aubrey, Cleaves, and Bada (2008)\n- **Title**: \"The Role of Submarine Hydrothermal Systems in the Synthesis of Amino Acids\"\n- **Journal**: Origins of Life and Evolution of Biospheres\n- **DOI**: 10.1007/s11084-008-9153-2\n- **Key finding**: Investigated amino acid synthesis under simulated submarine hydrothermal vent conditions. Demonstrated that L-alanine reacts with formaldehyde to produce L-threonine via aldol condensation. This reaction extends the carbon skeleton of a simpler amino acid (alanine, C3) to a more complex one (threonine, C4) by incorporating a C1 unit (formaldehyde). The same study demonstrated the analogous glycine + formaldehyde \u2192 serine aldol condensation, as well as serine reduction to alanine. These reactions establish a hydrothermal amino acid interconversion network where simpler amino acids serve as building blocks for more complex ones through aldol chemistry with formaldehyde.\n\n## 6. Environmental Context\n\n### Surface Environment (Primary for Pathway 1)\nThe cyanosulfidic threonine synthesis (Patel et al., 2015) operates under surface conditions:\n- **Cyanosulfidic nitrile chemistry**: Lactaldehyde reacts with HCN to form a cyanohydrin, which is aminated by ammonia and then hydrolyzed to threonine. This operates in surface pools where HCN-derived chemistry (UV-driven, evaporitic) produces aminonitrile intermediates that hydrolyze in wet phases.\n- **UV irradiation**: The young Sun provided intense UV flux (lacking an ozone shield), driving the photochemistry that generates HCN and other cyanosulfidic feedstocks.\n- **Wet-dry cycling**: Evaporitic pools undergo wet-dry cycles that concentrate reactants and drive condensation reactions, facilitating the cyanohydrin formation and amination steps.\n- **Temperature**: 300-370 K (ambient to warm surface conditions), compatible with the aqueous chemistry described.\n\n### Hydrothermal Environment (Primary for Pathway 2)\nThe aldol condensation pathway (Aubrey et al., 2008) operates under submarine hydrothermal conditions:\n- **L-Alanine availability**: L-alanine is produced by iron-catalyzed reductive amination of pyruvate at 70\u00b0C (Muchowska et al., 2019), by reduction of L-serine (Aubrey et al., 2008), and by cyanosulfidic aminonitrile hydrolysis (Patel et al., 2015). Multiple upstream pathways feed alanine into the hydrothermal environment.\n- **Formaldehyde availability**: Formaldehyde is produced by hydrothermal reduction of CO2 and by Fischer-Tropsch-type chemistry on native iron and nickel-iron mineral surfaces. It is a key C1 building block abundantly available in hydrothermal vent fluids.\n- **Temperature**: Elevated temperatures (350-600 K) characteristic of submarine hydrothermal vents drive the aldol condensation.\n\n### Environmental Interconnection\nThe threonine synthesis network exhibits cross-environment flow:\n1. **Surface \u2192 Hydrothermal**: L-Alanine produced by cyanosulfidic chemistry (Pathway 1-type aminonitrile hydrolysis in surface pools) could be transported to hydrothermal environments, where it undergoes aldol condensation with formaldehyde (Pathway 2).\n2. **Hydrothermal \u2192 Surface**: Alanine and formaldehyde generated at hydrothermal vents could be transported to surface environments via ocean circulation.\n3. **Parallel independent routes**: The two pathways operate independently in different environments (surface cyanosulfidic vs. hydrothermal aldol), providing robustness to the prebiotic threonine synthesis network.\n4. **Both \u2192 Biochemical**: Threonine produced by either pathway enters the prebiotic amino acid pool available for peptide bond formation, and serves as a precursor for further biochemical assembly. Threonine itself can thermally decompose to glycine under hydrothermal conditions (Vallentyne, 1964; Bada et al., 1978), linking it to the broader amino acid recycling network.\n\n## 7. Open Questions and Challenges\n\n1. **Chirality**: Both demonstrated prebiotic routes produce racemic threonine (equal L and D forms). The origin of homochirality (biological preference for L-amino acids) remains unresolved. Mineral surface adsorption, photolysis with circularly polarized light, or amplification through autocatalytic crystallization have been proposed but not demonstrated specifically for threonine.\n\n2. **Lactaldehyde availability**: Pathway 1 requires lactaldehyde (2-hydroxypropionaldehyde), whose prebiotic abundance and stability are debated. It could form from aldol chemistry of simpler aldehydes (glycolaldehyde + formaldehyde), from sugar photochemistry under cyanosulfidic conditions, or from reduction of pyruvaldehyde. Quantitative constraints on its steady-state concentration in surface environments are needed.\n\n3. **Threonine thermal stability**: Threonine is thermally labile and decomposes to glycine under hydrothermal conditions (Vallentyne, 1964; Bada et al., 1978). The balance between threonine production (via aldol condensation, Pathway 2) and thermal decomposition at hydrothermal temperatures is a key kinetic question. The retro-aldol decomposition of threonine to glycine (the reverse of the serine-type aldol reaction) may limit threonine accumulation in hydrothermal systems.\n\n4. **2-Amino-3-hydroxybutanenitrile stability**: Pathway 1 requires this aminonitrile intermediate to persist long enough for hydrolysis. Its stability under UV irradiation, varying pH, and temperature fluctuations in surface pools needs further study. Competing reactions (polymerization, reaction with other HCN-derived species) could reduce yields.\n\n5. **Formaldehyde concentration**: Pathway 2 requires formaldehyde, which is volatile and reactive. Its prebiotic concentration in hydrothermal environments is debated. Mineral adsorption, continuous production from CO2 reduction via serpentinization-derived H2, and thermophoretic concentration may maintain sufficient steady-state levels.\n\n6. **Alanine as precursor**: Pathway 2 requires L-alanine as a substrate, which must itself be produced by upstream prebiotic reactions. The availability of alanine in sufficient concentrations at hydrothermal vents, where it is simultaneously being consumed by aldol condensation and potentially decomposed at high temperatures, represents a kinetic bottleneck that needs quantitative modeling.\n\n7. **Selectivity and side products**: The aldol condensation of alanine with formaldehyde (Pathway 2) could potentially produce other isomeric products besides threonine. The stereoselectivity and regioselectivity of this reaction under prebiotic conditions, and the relative yields of threonine versus allo-threonine or other isomers, need further characterization.\n\n=== END CURATED LITERATURE REVIEW ===\n\n=== END LITERATURE RESEARCH ===\n\nConsider how well the network aligns with this literature in your evaluation.\n\n\nPlease evaluate the network and respond with JSON:\n{\n    \"pathway_evaluations\": [\n        {\n            \"pathway_id\": \"<id>\",\n            \"reaction_scores\": {\"rxn_001\": 0.85, ...},\n            \"chemical_feasibility\": <0.0-1.0>,\n            \"pathway_coherence\": <0.0-1.0>,\n            \"environmental_progression\": <0.0-1.0>,\n            \"target_achievement\": <0.0-1.0>,\n            \"overall_confidence\": <0.0-1.0>,\n            \"feedback\": \"<pathway-specific feedback>\"